1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:05,580
The World Trade Center, unmistakable in its silhouette against the sky.

2
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:12,400
Its twin towers of steel, glass, and concrete reach more than 1,360 feet in the air,

3
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:16,060
dominating the skyline as Manhattan's tallest structures.

4
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:22,220
The pair has come to symbolize both the contemporary spirit and vitality of New York.

5
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:27,680
I believe New York's icon at the moment is the World Trade Center.

6
00:00:27,681 --> 00:00:34,760
It's in every major picture around the world as symbolizing New York.

7
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:38,660
I think anyone who studies it on a certain level falls in love with the World Trade Center.

8
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:43,860
No matter what you think about it aesthetically, no matter what you think about it politically or culturally,

9
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:46,000
no matter how you think it looks on the skyline,

10
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:51,920
the fact that human beings have the ingenuity to be able to do this kind of thing

11
00:00:51,921 --> 00:00:56,240
is, you know, kind of stunning and affirming in an odd kind of way.

12
00:00:56,241 --> 00:01:01,240
Nothing else like this had ever been built before or since.

13
00:01:01,241 --> 00:01:09,240
And I think the beauty, the architectural beauty of the World Trade Center is subtle and it's easy to underestimate.

14
00:01:09,241 --> 00:01:19,240
Each tower is 110 stories high, each floor an acre in size, under each roof 4 million square feet of space.

15
00:01:19,241 --> 00:01:25,240
But the towers are merely the focal point of what is an extraordinary complex of seven buildings.

16
00:01:25,241 --> 00:01:30,960
In all, there are more than 12 million square feet of enclosed space.

17
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:40,060
You have to imagine a small town concentrated vertically rather than spread out over 1,000 acres.

18
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,400
It's concentrated on a 16-acre site.

19
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:45,600
40,000 people work here.

20
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:48,140
Another 2 million visit each year.

21
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:52,480
And although the complex was built to be a center for international trade,

22
00:01:52,481 --> 00:01:57,600
The reality is that few businesses here have much to do with foreign trade.

23
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:03,200
Some 325 companies, from stockbrokers to high-tech firms,

24
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:06,820
share this prestigious address with the U.S. Customs House,

25
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:11,940
a Marriott Hotel, and Manhattan's largest indoor shopping mall.

26
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:15,720
Subway lines intersect here at the World Trade Center,

27
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:21,900
so people can come in underground, never get wet, never get snowed upon,

28
00:02:21,901 --> 00:02:26,080
go to their office, go downstairs, do their shopping, grab lunch, come back to their office,

29
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:29,060
and go home underground, and you never go outside.

30
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:30,480
So it's a city within a city.

31
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:37,680
And supporting the city within a city is an elaborate infrastructure, dependable and vigilant.

32
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:43,600
There's a whole building that exists inside of the World Trade Center that nobody gets to see

33
00:02:43,601 --> 00:02:49,400
because it's what runs the building, including all the stuff downstairs,

34
00:02:49,401 --> 00:02:53,740
The massive systems, electrical, air conditioning and other,

35
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:56,480
that actually allow us to sit here

36
00:02:56,481 --> 00:02:58,980
pretending that we're in a natural environment.

37
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:03,280
Nothing could be less natural than a super tall building.

38
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:06,480
Nothing could be less natural for its time

39
00:03:06,481 --> 00:03:08,420
than a building like the World Trade Center.

40
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:15,780
So vast is this city,

41
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:18,560
the World Trade Center has its own police force

42
00:03:18,561 --> 00:03:21,940
and what it calls the Operations Control Center, or OCC.

43
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:25,480
The department's sole responsibility

44
00:03:25,481 --> 00:03:29,240
is to keep this mini-metropolis safe, clean, and functioning.

45
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:32,960
The Operation Control Center of the World Trade Center

46
00:03:32,961 --> 00:03:35,840
could be compared to the war room on board a warship.

47
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:38,420
It's manned 24 hours a day, 7 days a week,

48
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:39,800
and they respond to crises.

49
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:43,300
The OCC can be found in the sub-basement

50
00:03:43,301 --> 00:03:45,160
in a series of windowless offices.

51
00:03:45,161 --> 00:03:51,240
Whatever goes on here at the World Trade Center is typically coordinated through that room.

52
00:03:51,340 --> 00:03:55,820
If a tenant has a flood on their floor from an air conditioner overflowing, that's where they call.

53
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:58,740
If the fire department needs a security guard to open up doors,

54
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:02,820
they're the ones that are sending the security people with the right keys to open up the door.

55
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:06,660
More than 30,000 work orders are handled each year.

56
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:11,260
The OCC monitors all emergency, water and electrical systems,

57
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:15,060
including the World Trade Center's immense refrigeration unit.

58
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:20,560
I was overwhelmed with the size and scope of the machinery.

59
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:25,640
It was larger than the flight deck of an aircraft carrier.

60
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:32,560
Just huge pipes on a scale which reminds one of Gulliver's travel,

61
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:36,800
so one feels like a Lilliputian among such huge machinery.

62
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:42,840
Four basements below the Twin Towers on 2.5 acres of concrete

63
00:04:42,841 --> 00:04:46,360
is the heart of the World Trade Center's air conditioning system.

64
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:53,240
It's a system so massive, it can filter and circulate 9 million cubic feet of air per minute.

65
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:56,920
We're roughly 60 feet below grade.

66
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:02,360
We have seven heat exchangers.

67
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:04,280
They're some of the largest in the world.

68
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:09,900
Each heat exchanger has a capacity of 7,000 tons.

69
00:05:09,901 --> 00:05:13,900
The equipment is used to cool both towers.

70
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:17,980
They're sister buildings, the shopping concourse, and the subway station.

71
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:21,800
The first subway station to be air-conditioned in the United States.

72
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:30,920
We bring in river water from the Hudson, and it runs through the heat exchangers

73
00:05:30,921 --> 00:05:35,720
and cools the water that we use for air conditioning.

74
00:05:35,721 --> 00:05:41,540
Engineers of the World Trade Center had considered conventional rooftop cooling towers,

75
00:05:41,541 --> 00:05:46,280
but the units would have been so enormous there was concern that they would mar the appearance

76
00:05:46,281 --> 00:05:49,055
of the building. The solution lay nearby.

77
00:05:49,056 --> 00:05:52,100
Because the complex sits by the Hudson River,

78
00:05:52,101 --> 00:05:57,440
cold water could be pumped directly from the river and used to cool the massive system.

79
00:05:57,441 --> 00:06:04,240
Today, large intake pipes provide the refrigeration plant with 90,000 gallons of

80
00:06:04,241 --> 00:06:09,400
water per minute. River water is run through the system and then vented back

81
00:06:09,401 --> 00:06:13,340
out to the Hudson River. Of course the Environmental Protection Agency keeps a

82
00:06:13,341 --> 00:06:17,280
close watch because they don't want too fierce of a temperature gradient because

83
00:06:17,281 --> 00:06:23,180
of the flora and fauna in the river itself. The World Trade Center complex

84
00:06:23,181 --> 00:06:31,300
has 71 escalators and 254 elevators that make as many as 450,000 trips a day.

85
00:06:31,301 --> 00:06:38,100
The express elevators can travel at speeds of up to 27 feet per second, or 19 miles per hour.

86
00:06:38,101 --> 00:06:40,652
The elevators are quite powerful.

87
00:06:40,653 --> 00:06:45,600
Each elevator has its own motor and its own motor generator set,

88
00:06:45,601 --> 00:06:50,300
generating about 480 horsepower.

89
00:06:50,301 --> 00:06:55,600
In case of a power failure, the World Trade Center has six emergency generators.

90
00:06:56,480 --> 00:07:00,060
This backup system can yield 7,200 kilowatts,

91
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:03,940
enough to satisfy the needs of 4,000 suburban homes.

92
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:07,780
It's just enough to power an elevator bank in each tower,

93
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:11,580
stairway lighting, fire sprinklers, and other emergency essentials.

94
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:20,260
In addition to that, we have now built a large diesel power plant

95
00:07:20,261 --> 00:07:23,120
on top of Five World Trade Center,

96
00:07:23,121 --> 00:07:28,320
where we not only can provide emergency power to the facility,

97
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:35,120
but we can provide emergency backup power to all of the tenants' computer needs.

98
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:42,280
Even washing the 600,000 square feet of windows of the Twin Towers requires something out of the ordinary,

99
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:46,000
something dreamed up by its architect, Minuro Yamasaki,

100
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:51,720
who was afraid of heights and did not want to have workers hanging over the side while washing the building.

101
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:57,120
Automatic window washers were custom designed for each tower.

102
00:07:57,121 --> 00:08:01,120
Attached by cable and controlled by an operator,

103
00:08:01,121 --> 00:08:04,120
each moves along a rail at the top of the building

104
00:08:04,121 --> 00:08:08,120
and down miniature tracks embedded in the exterior columns.

105
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:15,120
The window washing machines are marvelous pieces of machinery.

106
00:08:15,121 --> 00:08:19,120
Each one has a set of nylon brushes.

107
00:08:19,121 --> 00:08:19,845
brushes.

108
00:08:19,846 --> 00:08:27,000
Two brushes, 18 and a half inches, exactly how much is windows from 106 all the

109
00:08:27,001 --> 00:08:29,273
way down to the ninth floor.

110
00:08:29,274 --> 00:08:33,900
And a squeegee and a 20 gallon tank which holds a dose of

111
00:08:33,901 --> 00:08:34,746
detergent.

112
00:08:34,747 --> 00:08:41,600
And these machines go up and down the sides of the buildings between the columns,

113
00:08:41,601 --> 00:08:44,255
cleaning one column of windows at a time.

114
00:08:44,256 --> 00:08:47,040
I clean with this machine half an hour, one

115
00:08:47,041 --> 00:08:53,769
under windows for half an hour. 20 minutes going down washing slow speed.

116
00:08:53,770 --> 00:08:55,520
10 minutes comes up

117
00:08:56,480 --> 00:09:02,680
on fast speed. Then I move to one column to the other and send the washer again.

118
00:09:02,681 --> 00:09:03,920
For half an hour

119
00:09:03,921 --> 00:09:06,281
I am free. The washer does everything.

120
00:09:06,282 --> 00:09:09,760
The machine takes a full day to clean 12 window columns,

121
00:09:09,761 --> 00:09:12,920
five days to clean an entire side.

122
00:09:13,680 --> 00:09:15,060
When one side is finished,

123
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:19,120
turntables on the roof guide the machine in position for the next side.

124
00:09:20,980 --> 00:09:24,980
In a month's time, it will have done all four sides

125
00:09:24,981 --> 00:09:26,940
and repeats the process.

126
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:31,880
This is done just like clockwork from March to December.

127
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:33,940
However, during the winter months,

128
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:37,160
the operation is suspended because the water would freeze.

129
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:42,040
Every corner of this synthetic city reveals the unexpected.

130
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:45,560
Underground, as congested as any street in Manhattan.

131
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:50,180
A loading dock where hundreds of trucks jostle for space to unload deliveries.

132
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:57,860
1,377 feet above, on the South Tower, the world's tallest outdoor viewing platform.

133
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:04,520
On clear days, visibility is 45 miles, with dazzling views of New York and New Jersey.

134
00:10:04,521 --> 00:10:11,960
Today, the World Trade Center has become an icon for success, ambition, and Manhattan

135
00:10:11,961 --> 00:10:12,661
itself.

136
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:15,200
We see it in ads and on television.

137
00:10:15,940 --> 00:10:20,580
We've become so familiar with it that it's now entwined with the New York of our imagination.

138
00:10:21,860 --> 00:10:26,580
And remarkably, after all this time, it continues to inspire awe.

139
00:10:26,581 --> 00:10:39,721
But the sheer size of them, I think, represent a moment in history that is lost today and that we can only look back and be impressed with the ambition and almost

140
00:10:39,722 --> 00:10:42,560
heroism of the project of the time.

141
00:10:43,560 --> 00:10:51,960
Uniform in color, but constantly changing with a light, the towers of the World Trade Center are among the most distinctive on the globe.

142
00:10:51,961 --> 00:10:58,960
To conceive something on this scale would take a diligent core of visionaries and engineers.

143
00:11:01,960 --> 00:11:07,360
Now that the World Trade Center is gone, we still think of heroism, but for a different reason.

144
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:15,380
Because of the tragic events of September 11th, now we think of the hundreds of firemen, policemen, EMS and Port Authority workers

145
00:11:15,381 --> 00:11:18,320
who ran toward the danger, not away from it.

146
00:11:18,321 --> 00:11:22,137
And we should also think of two of the men you just met, Mr.

147
00:11:22,138 --> 00:11:27,480
Rocco Camage, the man who operated that Rube Goldberg of a window-washing mechanism,

148
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:28,460
and Mr.

149
00:11:28,461 --> 00:11:36,260
Frank DiMartini, the construction manager who explained the marvels of the World Trade Center's air conditioning and power systems to us.

150
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:39,120
Both men are listed among the missing.

151
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:45,800
But no one could have known what the terrible fate of those twin towers would be when they were first built.

152
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:49,640
They seemed as strong as America itself.

153
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:54,260
Completed in 1974,

154
00:11:55,140 --> 00:11:58,360
the Twin Towers remained the tallest buildings in New York City.

155
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:00,480
That's a venerable accomplishment,

156
00:12:00,780 --> 00:12:03,560
given the city's long history of erecting skyscrapers

157
00:12:03,561 --> 00:12:05,220
to rival those that came before.

158
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:09,240
But neither the World Trade Center nor any other tall building

159
00:12:09,241 --> 00:12:12,380
would have been possible without two engineering developments.

160
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:14,320
First, the elevator.

161
00:12:14,321 --> 00:12:17,320
You can't have a tall office building without the elevator.

162
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:20,140
And the first commercial building that was built with an elevator

163
00:12:20,141 --> 00:12:23,180
was the Equitable Life Assurance Company's headquarters on Broadway,

164
00:12:23,540 --> 00:12:25,000
built in the late 1860s.

165
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:31,140
The next major advance was the development of the steel skeleton frame,

166
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:35,880
which occurs in 1888 when the tower building is built,

167
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:39,120
which was probably the world's first building with a steel skeleton frame.

168
00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:42,280
With the steel skeleton and the elevator,

169
00:12:42,281 --> 00:12:46,080
the Manhattan skyline began an extraordinary transformation.

170
00:12:47,220 --> 00:12:51,260
Within only a few years after the tower building was completed in the late 1880s,

171
00:12:51,660 --> 00:12:57,600
you have enormous skyscrapers being built that were rising 20, 30 stories.

172
00:12:58,580 --> 00:13:03,320
These would not have been possible either if the economics hadn't changed,

173
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:08,280
if banking and commerce and trade and the insurance businesses weren't growing.

174
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:11,640
During the first decades of the 20th century,

175
00:13:12,260 --> 00:13:14,720
some of New York's most famous skyscrapers were built.

176
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:17,880
In 1903, the Flatiron Fuller Building.

177
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:21,220
In 1909, the Metropolitan Life Tower.

178
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:23,980
And in 1913, the Woolworth Building.

179
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:28,160
The next great growth of skyscrapers occurred in the 1920s.

180
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:33,040
It culminated in 1930 and 31 with buildings like the Chrysler Building

181
00:13:33,041 --> 00:13:34,700
and, of course, the Empire State Building,

182
00:13:34,701 --> 00:13:36,820
which was the world's tallest building

183
00:13:36,821 --> 00:13:39,480
for the 40 years before the World Trade Center.

184
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:44,420
During each one of those cycles, there was a boom in the economy

185
00:13:44,421 --> 00:13:47,320
that drove the heights of buildings ever taller.

186
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:52,760
It was during the euphoric boom years immediately following World War II

187
00:13:52,761 --> 00:13:58,320
that New York lawmakers conceived the idea of an international or world trade center.

188
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:01,980
They expected an increase in global trade

189
00:14:01,981 --> 00:14:05,780
and believed New York could be more competitive with other port cities

190
00:14:05,781 --> 00:14:10,120
if all businesses related to trade were housed under one roof.

191
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:15,160
The idea came about that there would be a big trade mart

192
00:14:15,161 --> 00:14:18,500
based on the ancient fair in Leipzig,

193
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:20,600
which went back to the Middle Ages.

194
00:14:20,700 --> 00:14:25,960
There would be a fair, a permanent trade exposition in New York

195
00:14:25,961 --> 00:14:31,340
where people from all over the world could come and show their goods.

196
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:33,880
But when market research revealed

197
00:14:33,881 --> 00:14:36,760
that the city would benefit more by modernizing its ports,

198
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:38,540
the idea was scrapped.

199
00:14:39,700 --> 00:14:42,140
It wouldn't resurface again for more than a decade,

200
00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:45,560
and only then, under the aegis of urban renewal

201
00:14:45,561 --> 00:14:48,600
and the influence of one of New York's most famous heirs,

202
00:14:49,460 --> 00:14:50,300
David Rockefeller,

203
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:52,880
grandson of Standard Oil founder and billionaire

204
00:14:52,881 --> 00:14:54,280
John D. Rockefeller.

205
00:14:56,160 --> 00:14:59,180
In 1958, Rockefeller spearheaded

206
00:14:59,181 --> 00:15:01,280
what was called the Billion Dollar Plan

207
00:15:01,281 --> 00:15:04,380
to stimulate business and revitalize Lower Manhattan.

208
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:09,560
His vision was to spend a billion dollars of private and public monies

209
00:15:09,561 --> 00:15:12,440
to create a kind of gleaming futuropolis.

210
00:15:13,340 --> 00:15:16,460
The core of this thing is going to be this great public purpose,

211
00:15:16,840 --> 00:15:21,360
a world trade center that will secure the future of New York City's port forever.

212
00:15:21,361 --> 00:15:25,200
So they said, well, here's all this stuff we can demolish over on the east side.

213
00:15:25,860 --> 00:15:29,720
This, by the way, is where the South Street Seaport is today.

214
00:15:29,721 --> 00:15:31,960
All the preserved buildings are today.

215
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:34,320
But at that point, it was considered fair game.

216
00:15:34,440 --> 00:15:36,580
They're 19th century buildings. Knock them down.

217
00:15:36,820 --> 00:15:40,560
It should also be pointed out that David Rockefeller's brother

218
00:15:40,561 --> 00:15:43,840
was Nelson Rockefeller, who was the governor of New York.

219
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:46,340
So this is very helpful in pushing the project through.

220
00:15:46,760 --> 00:15:50,520
In fact, some people have said that the Twin Towers

221
00:15:50,521 --> 00:15:52,840
should be renamed David and Nelson.

222
00:15:53,660 --> 00:15:55,800
To fund and build the new project,

223
00:15:55,801 --> 00:15:58,980
David Rockefeller and his supporters turned to the Port Authority.

224
00:15:59,720 --> 00:16:03,880
an agency chartered in 1921 by the states of New York and New Jersey

225
00:16:03,881 --> 00:16:07,440
to build and operate any terminal or transportation facility

226
00:16:07,441 --> 00:16:10,480
within a 25-mile radius of the Statue of Liberty.

227
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:15,040
The Port Authority is a hybrid creature,

228
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:19,960
partly a government agency and partly operating as a private business.

229
00:16:19,961 --> 00:16:25,800
No one else had quite the same combination of deep pockets and financial resources,

230
00:16:25,801 --> 00:16:31,500
the right of eminent domain, and the technical engineering skills to launch a big project.

231
00:16:32,580 --> 00:16:39,480
The other great power that the Port Authority has stems from the nature of public authorities in general.

232
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:44,360
If you want to build a project that costs a billion dollars, you simply sell bonds to do that,

233
00:16:44,420 --> 00:16:48,900
and then you charge rents or tolls or something like that to pay those bonds off.

234
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:54,020
The self-supporting agency built, owned, and operated several megastructures,

235
00:16:54,021 --> 00:16:57,520
such as the Lincoln Tunnel and the George Washington Bridge.

236
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:02,240
By the early 1960s, the World Trade Center project was added to its portfolio

237
00:17:02,241 --> 00:17:05,700
under the auspices of Port Authority director Austin J. Tobin.

238
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:11,700
There was a kind of professionalism that the Port Authority was known for

239
00:17:11,701 --> 00:17:15,240
in its engineering and its planning and its whole way of carrying things out,

240
00:17:15,380 --> 00:17:18,600
which was really impeccable, really impeccable.

241
00:17:18,700 --> 00:17:22,940
And Tobin was the guy who did that, and he was the central point of control.

242
00:17:22,941 --> 00:17:24,140
He was the Sun King.

243
00:17:25,440 --> 00:17:27,880
It was decided, okay, it's going to be a large project.

244
00:17:28,020 --> 00:17:29,400
It's going to be done by the Port Authority.

245
00:17:29,680 --> 00:17:31,900
It's going to be concerned with world trade.

246
00:17:32,180 --> 00:17:33,160
So far, so good.

247
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:38,940
The original plan was to put it on the east side of lower Manhattan.

248
00:17:39,580 --> 00:17:43,580
Bear in mind that the Port Authority is a bi-state agency.

249
00:17:44,020 --> 00:17:47,400
So understandably, the governor of New Jersey was concerned.

250
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:50,820
He said, well, you're building this World Trade Center for New York.

251
00:17:50,821 --> 00:17:52,180
What are you going to do for us?

252
00:17:52,940 --> 00:17:58,140
The Port Authority agreed to take over New Jersey's bankrupt Hudson and Manhattan Commuter Railroad,

253
00:17:58,480 --> 00:18:01,400
now known as PATH for Port Authority Trans-Hudson.

254
00:18:02,040 --> 00:18:06,800
The railroad had been running since 1908 and was in dire need of a facelift.

255
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:10,580
Its terminus was on the west side of Lower Manhattan.

256
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:17,460
Once this deal was in place, that New Jersey was getting the PATH, New York was getting the Trade Center,

257
00:18:18,260 --> 00:18:20,460
gradually it hit everyone.

258
00:18:20,461 --> 00:18:22,700
Why not put it on the west side?

259
00:18:22,980 --> 00:18:25,100
And then he could do two projects at once.

260
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:27,300
It was a brilliant breakthrough.

261
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:29,620
And the governor of New Jersey said,

262
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:31,260
at least we can see the damn thing.

263
00:18:31,940 --> 00:18:35,140
The World Trade Center project was moved from the east side

264
00:18:35,141 --> 00:18:37,660
to the middle of a neighborhood known as Radio Row.

265
00:18:38,240 --> 00:18:40,700
This was the center of the radio industry,

266
00:18:40,960 --> 00:18:44,000
where I remember going down there as a child with my father

267
00:18:44,001 --> 00:18:45,200
to buy radio tubes.

268
00:18:45,201 --> 00:18:52,300
It was a sort of a bazaar of retailers,

269
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:56,100
mostly involving consumer electronics of some kind.

270
00:18:56,740 --> 00:19:02,240
And this was where the first TVs were sold off the shelves in the United States.

271
00:19:02,340 --> 00:19:04,900
I mean, this was the consumer appliance mecca.

272
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:09,100
Radio Row merchants protested,

273
00:19:09,420 --> 00:19:12,100
but their shops would vanish by way of the wrecking ball

274
00:19:12,101 --> 00:19:15,520
as plans to build the biggest complex in Lower Manhattan geared up.

275
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:20,080
Ultimately, they didn't have much of an argument

276
00:19:20,081 --> 00:19:25,900
when you compared what, in the minds of the various judges

277
00:19:25,901 --> 00:19:28,080
who heard the cases, was the public good.

278
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:29,940
Well, I mean, you're just a business.

279
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:31,840
You can go and you can move someplace else.

280
00:19:32,700 --> 00:19:34,000
But this is Lower Manhattan,

281
00:19:34,760 --> 00:19:37,620
and the financial district has to expand.

282
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:39,780
I mean, look, you guys got to get out of the way.

283
00:19:40,780 --> 00:19:42,580
The merchants were bought out.

284
00:19:43,400 --> 00:19:47,180
Planners decided the World Trade Center site would occupy 16 acres,

285
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:49,920
but it was not yet known who would design it.

286
00:19:50,680 --> 00:19:53,880
When Yammer received the letter, we looked at the letter,

287
00:19:54,460 --> 00:19:56,700
and Yammer kind of called us together and said,

288
00:19:57,220 --> 00:19:59,200
you know, it just shows how careful you've got to be

289
00:19:59,201 --> 00:20:01,040
when you write a letter of invitation like this,

290
00:20:01,041 --> 00:20:04,560
because here's a project that obviously has a misplaced decimal point.

291
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:05,940
It's got too many zeros.

292
00:20:05,941 --> 00:20:09,840
who would be asking our firm to do a project of $250 million.

293
00:20:10,240 --> 00:20:12,680
But we prevailed upon Yamaha to follow through and make a call.

294
00:20:13,380 --> 00:20:15,640
It was the call that changed his life.

295
00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:19,180
Out of a field of more than a dozen prominent architects,

296
00:20:19,820 --> 00:20:23,260
Austin Tobin and the Port Authority chose Minoru Yamasaki

297
00:20:23,261 --> 00:20:24,600
and his firm out of Michigan

298
00:20:24,601 --> 00:20:27,720
to design one of the most important buildings in the world.

299
00:20:28,520 --> 00:20:30,420
The year was 1962.

300
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:35,760
Yamasaki was a rather strange choice as an architect.

301
00:20:35,940 --> 00:20:39,000
because he had never built anything on this scale before,

302
00:20:39,180 --> 00:20:45,060
and most of his previous buildings had been rather precious boxes,

303
00:20:45,640 --> 00:20:50,340
buildings that were detailed and ornamented in a way

304
00:20:50,341 --> 00:20:54,380
that you wouldn't expect to relate to the size of the World Trade Center.

305
00:20:54,381 --> 00:21:00,640
He was also famously a strange pick because he had fear of heights.

306
00:21:01,760 --> 00:21:04,880
What impressed the Port Authority was Yamasaki's grasp of engineering

307
00:21:04,881 --> 00:21:06,000
and his character.

308
00:21:06,680 --> 00:21:08,100
He was a self-made man,

309
00:21:08,520 --> 00:21:10,800
rising from a hardscrabble childhood in Seattle

310
00:21:10,801 --> 00:21:12,900
to acclaim as an award-winning architect.

311
00:21:13,620 --> 00:21:16,740
They saw someone who was similar in some ways

312
00:21:16,741 --> 00:21:18,180
to them and to their values.

313
00:21:18,620 --> 00:21:20,840
This was not an architectural prima donna.

314
00:21:21,700 --> 00:21:23,560
Yamasaki and his team went to work

315
00:21:23,561 --> 00:21:24,620
studying and designing

316
00:21:24,621 --> 00:21:26,740
over a hundred different schemes and models.

317
00:21:27,760 --> 00:21:30,760
There were times when something

318
00:21:30,761 --> 00:21:32,460
would just simply go wrong.

319
00:21:32,461 --> 00:21:37,680
And Yama would look at it and would see the flaws in it quickly and very compassionately say,

320
00:21:37,820 --> 00:21:39,080
let's try a little something else.

321
00:21:40,280 --> 00:21:45,280
Yamasaki finally settled on a two-tower development between 80 and 90 stories tall.

322
00:21:46,280 --> 00:21:49,500
Its plaza was evocative of the Piazza San Marco in Venice.

323
00:21:50,380 --> 00:21:56,700
Light poured into interior public areas, and windows were designed shoulder-width to give people a sense of security,

324
00:21:56,701 --> 00:21:58,680
no matter how high the floor.

325
00:21:58,681 --> 00:22:02,680
But the Port Authority wanted to make one important change.

326
00:22:02,681 --> 00:22:06,680
Given the square footage demands of the World Trade Center project,

327
00:22:06,681 --> 00:22:08,680
the Public Relations Department began to say,

328
00:22:08,681 --> 00:22:13,680
well, why not make it the tallest building in the world?

329
00:22:13,681 --> 00:22:19,680
He was very, very nervous about that because of the cost of pushing those extra floors in.

330
00:22:19,681 --> 00:22:23,680
He was fearful that the economics of the project would dictate

331
00:22:23,681 --> 00:22:26,680
to stripping away some of the things at the ground level

332
00:22:26,681 --> 00:22:32,300
level for the planting in the plaza, the flowers in the plaza, the sculpture in the plaza,

333
00:22:32,820 --> 00:22:37,980
and even in the lobbies, the amount of marble that's in the lobbies, the stainless steel doors,

334
00:22:38,180 --> 00:22:41,979
all of these things that he was so fond of. The budget was increased.

335
00:22:41,980 --> 00:22:43,080
Little, if anything,

336
00:22:43,081 --> 00:22:44,444
would be scaled back.

337
00:22:44,445 --> 00:22:49,120
And the famous 1,250-foot Empire State Building, the tallest building in

338
00:22:49,121 --> 00:22:56,300
the world since 1931, was about to be eclipsed by the new 1,360-foot towers of the World Trade Center.

339
00:22:56,680 --> 00:23:04,680
The World Trade Center was conceived back in the 1960s when I think American ambition

340
00:23:04,681 --> 00:23:08,680
and a faith in technology was really at its peak.

341
00:23:10,680 --> 00:23:16,680
If you think of the other sort of supersized topics of the day like jumbo jets,

342
00:23:16,681 --> 00:23:23,680
you have some sense of the sort of ambitious love of bigness that the World Trade Center represents.

343
00:23:26,680 --> 00:23:34,320
But to achieve that bigness, it would take engineering innovations and an army of workers manning revolutionary equipment.

344
00:23:36,060 --> 00:23:39,100
Welcome back to our eulogy to the World Trade Center.

345
00:23:39,640 --> 00:23:47,640
Even though it's gone now, we don't need to rely on programs like this one to remember it, to picture it towering majestically toward the heavens.

346
00:23:48,120 --> 00:23:54,400
It will exist as a part of the New York skyline in photographs, in paintings, and in films forever.

347
00:23:56,680 --> 00:24:03,740
Since it opened in 1931, the Empire State Building had held the title of the world's tallest building.

348
00:24:04,500 --> 00:24:09,380
Now, over 35 years later, the formidable task presented to engineers and architects

349
00:24:09,381 --> 00:24:14,940
was how to build a structure 100 feet taller than the Empire State Building, and do it twice.

350
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:19,380
This massive undertaking presented a number of unique and complex challenges.

351
00:24:20,240 --> 00:24:22,900
First, there was the matter of the building site itself.

352
00:24:22,901 --> 00:24:32,760
A major problem in the construction of the World Trade Center was the fact it was going to be built in Lower Manhattan in an area which was fundamentally fill.

353
00:24:32,980 --> 00:24:35,440
You have to think about the geology of the area.

354
00:24:35,880 --> 00:24:40,720
Lower Manhattan represents the confluence of two large rivers and the ocean.

355
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:47,020
Since the 1600s, the waterfront had been extended by over 700 feet by landfill operations.

356
00:24:47,780 --> 00:24:51,380
It had been filled with dirt, rubble, old piers and wharves.

357
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:56,020
To reach bedrock, engineers would have to dig down 65 feet

358
00:24:56,021 --> 00:25:00,060
and at the same time not disturb the foundations of the surrounding buildings.

359
00:25:00,760 --> 00:25:05,960
If you start to dig a hole, what happens is that if you only go down 3 feet,

360
00:25:06,380 --> 00:25:08,300
your hole fills up with water.

361
00:25:08,301 --> 00:25:13,620
They needed to dig not just a 3-foot hole, but a very, very large hole.

362
00:25:14,040 --> 00:25:18,000
It would have been the equivalent of pumping out the entire Hudson River.

363
00:25:18,740 --> 00:25:23,900
Not only that, even if you were able to pump all the water out,

364
00:25:24,180 --> 00:25:25,400
which is virtually impossible,

365
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,100
the surrounding buildings would have collapsed.

366
00:25:29,280 --> 00:25:33,000
The first obstacle, then, was how to control and remove the water

367
00:25:33,001 --> 00:25:35,480
while laying the foundation of the enormous structures.

368
00:25:37,300 --> 00:25:42,980
They had to come up with extremely unique and wonderful engineering techniques

369
00:25:42,981 --> 00:25:47,940
to actually pour that foundation while all this water was pressing in

370
00:25:47,941 --> 00:25:50,700
on the foundation structure itself.

371
00:25:51,420 --> 00:25:54,120
The solution came from engineer Ray Monte,

372
00:25:54,500 --> 00:25:56,960
who would utilize the slurry trench method.

373
00:25:57,560 --> 00:26:00,560
It had been used for subway construction in Europe and in Canada,

374
00:26:01,080 --> 00:26:03,920
and now would be used for the first time in the United States.

375
00:26:04,840 --> 00:26:10,180
For this project, excavating machines would dig a three-foot-wide trench down to bedrock.

376
00:26:10,700 --> 00:26:13,920
As dirt and rock were removed, they would be replaced by the slurry.

377
00:26:13,921 --> 00:26:21,420
The slurry is a mixture of water and bentonite, a type of expansive gray clay that has a swelling

378
00:26:21,421 --> 00:26:26,040
tendency and would plug any holes along the side as excavators dug deeper.

379
00:26:27,920 --> 00:26:30,695
The trench was dug in 22-foot segments.

380
00:26:30,696 --> 00:26:34,680
Then the workers lowered into each slurry-filled segment

381
00:26:34,681 --> 00:26:41,380
a pre-assembled seven-story high steel cage of reinforcing rods weighing 65 tons.

382
00:26:41,381 --> 00:26:46,720
The cage provided a framework for the concrete wall that was to come.

383
00:26:47,920 --> 00:26:50,740
The concrete would be poured in at the top of the trench

384
00:26:50,741 --> 00:26:53,560
through a pipe that resembled an elephant's trunk.

385
00:26:54,340 --> 00:26:58,320
The concrete was heavier than the clay, so it sank to the bottom,

386
00:26:58,540 --> 00:27:03,200
and the clay rose up and was reclaimed and used for the next section of trenching.

387
00:27:03,980 --> 00:27:09,860
All in all, construction workers had to make 152 of those 22-foot trench segments

388
00:27:09,861 --> 00:27:13,700
to enclose an area two blocks wide and four blocks long.

389
00:27:14,420 --> 00:27:18,580
It was like building a bathtub, only it's a bathtub in reverse.

390
00:27:19,160 --> 00:27:24,440
The purpose of this bathtub, 800 feet long, 200 feet wide, 70 feet deep,

391
00:27:24,780 --> 00:27:27,980
was not to contain water, but to keep water out.

392
00:27:28,140 --> 00:27:32,200
And the bathtub, you could have swum, Moby Dick could have done laps in the bathtub

393
00:27:32,201 --> 00:27:33,400
because it was that big.

394
00:27:33,940 --> 00:27:37,920
A unique method, and used extensively now.

395
00:27:37,921 --> 00:27:42,160
In fact, a number of people from other cities come to look at how the Trade Center was built,

396
00:27:42,380 --> 00:27:45,040
for example, in Denver, and are using the same method.

397
00:27:46,380 --> 00:27:48,880
The site posed another major challenge.

398
00:27:49,620 --> 00:27:54,480
The tunnels containing the path commuter rail lines lay underground within the excavation area.

399
00:27:55,540 --> 00:27:59,180
Over 130,000 passengers traveled daily on these lines.

400
00:28:00,180 --> 00:28:05,720
They had to dig around these extremely ancient cast iron tunnels for the Hudson tubes,

401
00:28:05,721 --> 00:28:06,820
what is now the path.

402
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:10,360
Port Authority Trans-Hudson commuter line.

403
00:28:11,100 --> 00:28:16,900
And these were really, really old, fragile, literally tubes,

404
00:28:17,020 --> 00:28:18,240
in which these trains ran.

405
00:28:18,900 --> 00:28:23,960
And to do the foundation work required almost microsurgery

406
00:28:23,961 --> 00:28:28,480
to excavate all around these and support them

407
00:28:28,481 --> 00:28:30,700
while they were doing all the foundation work

408
00:28:30,701 --> 00:28:32,340
and then raising the building over it.

409
00:28:32,680 --> 00:28:36,220
The fragile tubes were supported in protective cradles

410
00:28:36,221 --> 00:28:39,780
40 feet in diameter while excavation continued around them.

411
00:28:40,320 --> 00:28:43,680
We had to protect them over about four years

412
00:28:43,681 --> 00:28:50,120
while we were building from scratch to completion all the time.

413
00:28:50,260 --> 00:28:53,680
Kept them running, never created inconvenience for the passengers.

414
00:28:55,500 --> 00:28:57,460
Once excavation was complete,

415
00:28:57,980 --> 00:29:00,420
there was the burden of moving the excavated landfill.

416
00:29:01,180 --> 00:29:05,600
To remove the million cubic yards or 100,000 truckloads worth of landfill,

417
00:29:06,220 --> 00:29:10,340
The solution by Austin Tobin seemed to be a win-win situation.

418
00:29:12,820 --> 00:29:16,960
Instead of carting this dirt all the way to New Jersey or hauling it way out to sea,

419
00:29:17,340 --> 00:29:19,860
simply place it on the west side of West Street.

420
00:29:20,220 --> 00:29:25,900
But not just to dump it in the river, but to carefully contain it within a series of coffer dams,

421
00:29:25,901 --> 00:29:29,440
such as, in effect, you would be increasing the size of New York City.

422
00:29:29,820 --> 00:29:31,280
And that's precisely what they did.

423
00:29:31,281 --> 00:29:35,680
they measured out an area just west of West Street,

424
00:29:35,900 --> 00:29:39,400
which would extend 700 feet out into the Hudson River,

425
00:29:39,920 --> 00:29:42,400
be 1,400 feet long, in other words,

426
00:29:42,460 --> 00:29:45,160
about the size of six city blocks.

427
00:29:45,880 --> 00:29:49,320
They built a drum-shaped box of cellular cofferdams,

428
00:29:49,720 --> 00:29:52,740
a watertight enclosure to hold the material excavated

429
00:29:52,741 --> 00:29:53,740
from the building site.

430
00:29:53,741 --> 00:29:57,260
These were really like big sunken cylinders,

431
00:29:57,880 --> 00:29:59,740
each 63 feet in diameter,

432
00:29:59,741 --> 00:30:03,980
each one filled with 6,000 cubic yards of sand.

433
00:30:04,500 --> 00:30:10,780
So you would create this barrier 700 feet out to the river and 1,400 feet long,

434
00:30:10,900 --> 00:30:18,280
behind which you would put the excavated fill and thus create 23 acres of new land.

435
00:30:18,680 --> 00:30:24,700
As a result of the excavated material, the landfill created some of the most valuable real estate in the country.

436
00:30:25,500 --> 00:30:33,120
New York got $90 million worth of land that would eventually become Battery Park and the World Financial Center.

437
00:30:35,120 --> 00:30:42,380
Although the foundations were in place, there was still the problem of bringing thousands of tons of steel up over 100 floors.

438
00:30:43,360 --> 00:30:46,660
In past construction of skyscrapers, derricks were used.

439
00:30:47,340 --> 00:30:53,700
But a derrick had to be constantly lifted to a higher position, one floor at a time, costing several man hours.

440
00:30:54,700 --> 00:31:01,080
Derrick's also had to be powered from the ground, with extremely long cables running down through the floor already built.

441
00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:06,740
The answer was a self-powered crane that came from across the globe.

442
00:31:07,260 --> 00:31:12,280
These were the so-called kangaroo cranes, nicknamed because they're coming from Australia,

443
00:31:12,720 --> 00:31:15,380
and they were put at the corner of each building.

444
00:31:15,381 --> 00:31:28,385
And what these kangaroo cranes essentially were able to do was to hoist themselves up with automatic machinery built into the crane such that the crane could rise as

445
00:31:28,386 --> 00:31:30,040
the building went up.

446
00:31:30,860 --> 00:31:37,320
The crane's diesel motors supplied the power driving a series of hydraulic lifts installed at the base of each corner.

447
00:31:37,321 --> 00:31:46,080
an interesting problem at the very end is how do you get the kangaroo crane down

448
00:31:46,081 --> 00:31:50,940
and the way that was done is they had to be disassembled and brought down by elevator

449
00:31:50,941 --> 00:31:58,040
the cranes would raise a structure unlike any in the world before the buildings would be finished

450
00:31:58,041 --> 00:32:03,680
more radical construction and engineering methods would forever change how skyscrapers were built

451
00:32:03,681 --> 00:32:10,420
You may have just recognized one element to the World Trade Center that has become world famous.

452
00:32:10,920 --> 00:32:12,940
World famous for the worst of reasons.

453
00:32:12,941 --> 00:32:21,100
A large piece of the exterior skeleton, the skeleton that was part of the revolutionary construction methods used to build the Twin Towers,

454
00:32:21,600 --> 00:32:25,980
was the last element standing after the terrorist attack of September 11th.

455
00:32:26,560 --> 00:32:30,600
And although that last remaining upright structure has since been taken down,

456
00:32:30,601 --> 00:32:33,400
for the first few days after the tragedy,

457
00:32:33,860 --> 00:32:35,800
the image of a piece of that skeleton

458
00:32:35,801 --> 00:32:38,820
was in newspapers and on television around the world.

459
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:41,580
Also, a word of warning.

460
00:32:42,120 --> 00:32:45,160
You're going to hear from the late Mr. Frank Demartini again,

461
00:32:45,420 --> 00:32:47,660
and he's going to confidently describe

462
00:32:47,661 --> 00:32:50,480
how sturdy and secure the Twin Towers are,

463
00:32:50,840 --> 00:32:53,220
even if a plane were to crash into them.

464
00:32:53,820 --> 00:32:56,160
If you find that profoundly unnerving,

465
00:32:56,720 --> 00:32:58,160
you are not alone.

466
00:33:00,600 --> 00:33:06,040
The construction of the Twin Towers began in August 1968.

467
00:33:06,041 --> 00:33:10,100
Architect Minoru Yamasaki would move away from conventional techniques with a completely

468
00:33:10,101 --> 00:33:11,980
different type of skeleton.

469
00:33:11,981 --> 00:33:16,500
The common sense way of understanding the construction of a skyscraper, we think back

470
00:33:16,501 --> 00:33:20,380
to our childhood, is building it like an erector set.

471
00:33:20,381 --> 00:33:27,320
You have little pieces of steel which you hold together with rivets and grommets, and

472
00:33:27,321 --> 00:33:29,320
it's like building a cage.

473
00:33:29,321 --> 00:33:32,620
This is the normal way of building a steel skyscraper.

474
00:33:32,621 --> 00:33:34,880
It's a cage-like construction.

475
00:33:34,881 --> 00:33:41,380
You have vertical members and horizontal members, and you build little boxes, and you pile box

476
00:33:41,381 --> 00:33:46,120
upon box upon box, and thus you have a skyscraper.

477
00:33:46,121 --> 00:33:49,560
Yamasaki wanted something different.

478
00:33:49,561 --> 00:33:54,780
What he decided on was a revolutionary way of constructing high-rises, the exterior

479
00:33:54,781 --> 00:33:56,800
skeleton system.

480
00:33:56,801 --> 00:34:03,440
What Yamasaki came up with, in conjunction with structural engineers, is to make the

481
00:34:03,441 --> 00:34:11,900
skin of the building much stronger so that you wouldn't need internal columns.

482
00:34:11,901 --> 00:34:16,960
Ever since the 19th century, skyscrapers have been built with a network of interior-supporting

483
00:34:16,961 --> 00:34:22,740
columns. Their exterior walls were merely curtain walls that emitted light and kept

484
00:34:22,741 --> 00:34:23,486
out the rain.

485
00:34:23,487 --> 00:34:28,080
But in the World Trade Center, its tough exterior walls would carry the vertical

486
00:34:28,081 --> 00:34:32,280
loads and have the strength to resist the enormous force of lateral winds as well.

487
00:34:32,960 --> 00:34:37,240
High-strength steel that had not been available before made this possible.

488
00:34:38,160 --> 00:34:45,360
A good analogy, I think, to explain it is the World Trade Center towers are like stalks of celery.

489
00:34:45,361 --> 00:34:48,851
The rigidity comes from the outside, not from the inside.

490
00:34:48,852 --> 00:34:51,240
And this also accounts for their unique

491
00:34:51,241 --> 00:35:00,095
appearance. The building was designed to have a fully loaded 707 crash into it.

492
00:35:00,096 --> 00:35:01,440
That was the

493
00:35:01,441 --> 00:35:03,828
largest plane at the time.

494
00:35:03,829 --> 00:35:09,980
I believe that the building probably could sustain multiple impacts

495
00:35:09,981 --> 00:35:20,420
of jetliners because this structure is like the mosquito netting on your screen door, this intense

496
00:35:20,421 --> 00:35:26,222
grid and the jet plane is just a pencil puncturing that screen netting.

497
00:35:26,223 --> 00:35:28,020
It really does nothing

498
00:35:28,021 --> 00:35:30,160
to the screen netting.

499
00:35:30,161 --> 00:35:35,091
To achieve this kind of strength, engineers conducted two comprehensive tests.

500
00:35:35,092 --> 00:35:35,792
The first

501
00:35:35,793 --> 00:35:37,966
was a boundary layer wind tunnel test.

502
00:35:37,967 --> 00:35:40,940
Scale models of the World Trade Center along with

503
00:35:40,941 --> 00:35:44,359
a large section of the city were put into a wind tunnel.

504
00:35:44,360 --> 00:35:46,740
Planners spent hours doing mathematical

505
00:35:46,741 --> 00:35:49,580
stress calculations of various wind effects on the building.

506
00:35:50,100 --> 00:35:54,600
With the World Trade Center, it was the first time that for a high-rise building that

507
00:35:54,601 --> 00:35:58,960
comprehensive boundary layer wind tunnel studies had been performed.

508
00:35:59,740 --> 00:36:05,300
And previous to this, people had studied the effects of wind for aeronautical projects,

509
00:36:05,301 --> 00:36:10,700
for use in cars, designing cars, but for buildings that had not been used.

510
00:36:11,600 --> 00:36:16,400
Thirty years later, that type of testing is standard practice in the structural engineering industry.

511
00:36:16,740 --> 00:36:20,740
The second was a human sensitivity test.

512
00:36:20,741 --> 00:36:23,740
They created a very interesting experiment.

513
00:36:23,741 --> 00:36:28,740
The bait for this particular experiment is they were offering free eye examinations.

514
00:36:28,741 --> 00:36:32,740
And unbeknownst to the people, while they were getting their eye exam, it was a legitimate deal.

515
00:36:32,741 --> 00:36:34,740
They did get the eye exam.

516
00:36:34,741 --> 00:36:39,740
But what they didn't know was that the room in which the eye examination was taking place

517
00:36:39,741 --> 00:36:43,740
was being subjected to variable forces of sway.

518
00:36:43,741 --> 00:36:49,980
And what they were trying to determine is how much sway would the average person tolerate

519
00:36:49,981 --> 00:36:52,240
before he or she started to object.

520
00:36:52,241 --> 00:36:59,520
Amazingly, people tolerated sway equivalent to that caused by 150 mile per hour winds.

521
00:36:59,521 --> 00:37:04,880
The exterior skeleton system also allowed for a maximum of open, usable, and rentable

522
00:37:04,881 --> 00:37:07,740
column-free floor space.

523
00:37:07,741 --> 00:37:10,780
This helped create another engineering first.

524
00:37:10,781 --> 00:37:16,280
Two buildings, each 110 stories high, could have produced a maze of elevators.

525
00:37:16,281 --> 00:37:21,300
Tobin and Yamasaki avoided that problem by borrowing a key concept from New York's famed

526
00:37:21,301 --> 00:37:23,520
subway system.

527
00:37:23,521 --> 00:37:28,140
They proposed dividing each tower building into three parts or zones.

528
00:37:28,141 --> 00:37:32,680
Each zone would have its own lobby, and people would be able to transfer from express to

529
00:37:32,681 --> 00:37:36,000
local in the second and third zones.

530
00:37:36,001 --> 00:37:39,180
Initially the idea was almost shot down.

531
00:37:39,181 --> 00:37:42,440
Austin Tobin, who was the executive director of the Port Authority,

532
00:37:42,740 --> 00:37:46,340
had a blue ribbon panel of architectural experts.

533
00:37:47,220 --> 00:37:54,440
And the blue ribbon panel unanimously said that the tenants would never accept such an arrangement

534
00:37:54,441 --> 00:38:01,180
that had never been done before and that people would not tolerate the waste of time

535
00:38:01,181 --> 00:38:04,720
of taking two separate elevators to reach their destination.

536
00:38:04,721 --> 00:38:11,860
But Tobin continued to move forward and even asked Otis Elevator to build pioneering elevators

537
00:38:11,861 --> 00:38:14,720
with sliding doors in both the front and the rear.

538
00:38:15,400 --> 00:38:18,520
The first people in the elevator were also the first people out.

539
00:38:18,820 --> 00:38:24,720
As it turns out, people readily accepted it because the truth was that this system,

540
00:38:24,900 --> 00:38:27,760
even though it involved two rides, the express ride and the local ride,

541
00:38:28,020 --> 00:38:31,640
actually saved time and resulted in a shorter wait.

542
00:38:31,641 --> 00:38:35,460
We're essentially three buildings stacked one on top of another.

543
00:38:36,040 --> 00:38:40,160
A unique concept now used in numerous other super high-rise buildings around the world,

544
00:38:40,400 --> 00:38:44,160
whether it be Shanghai or Malaysia, but very innovative at its time.

545
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:46,640
During the building of the World Trade Center,

546
00:38:46,920 --> 00:38:52,080
each problem faced by engineers and contractors resulted in a revolutionary solution.

547
00:38:52,760 --> 00:38:57,580
This building is not just a machine, it's an intelligent machine or a smart machine.

548
00:38:57,581 --> 00:39:07,824
And one of the things that, you know, again, the Port Authority was way out ahead of everybody else in really marshalling the best, not just structural engineering

549
00:39:07,825 --> 00:39:12,160
minds, but also the best electrical and electronic engineering minds.

550
00:39:13,540 --> 00:39:19,680
All of these pioneering ideas created a complex that was structurally sound and incredibly efficient.

551
00:39:20,600 --> 00:39:26,100
Its strength in its very existence would be most severely tested not by high winds or a jet airplane,

552
00:39:26,820 --> 00:39:29,920
but by an unforeseen and appalling act.

553
00:39:33,420 --> 00:39:38,860
That terrorist act took place in 1993, an explosion that killed six people

554
00:39:38,861 --> 00:39:43,180
but left the twin towers of the World Trade Center essentially intact.

555
00:39:43,860 --> 00:39:47,700
What no one could foresee was the terrorist attack of September 11th.

556
00:39:47,701 --> 00:39:52,200
It destroyed both buildings entirely and killed thousands of innocent people.

557
00:39:52,900 --> 00:39:56,100
One of those innocent people was Mr. Frank Demartini.

558
00:39:56,760 --> 00:40:00,460
Both he and his wife were in his office when the first plane hit.

559
00:40:01,200 --> 00:40:06,120
He sent her down the stairs and safely out of the building while he stayed and tried to help others.

560
00:40:06,540 --> 00:40:10,440
Mr. Demartini is also the gentleman from whom you just heard,

561
00:40:10,840 --> 00:40:17,320
who correctly yet eerily pointed out that the World Trade Center could withstand a blow from a 707 jetliner.

562
00:40:17,700 --> 00:40:24,540
But physicists and engineers agree that no building, certainly no building anyone would want to work or live in,

563
00:40:24,860 --> 00:40:33,240
could withstand the intense heat generated by the burning jet fuel from a fully loaded 767 used as a weapon of mass destruction.

564
00:40:33,840 --> 00:40:39,400
Like the rest of us, the late Mr. DiMartini could never have imagined such a thing.

565
00:40:39,401 --> 00:40:49,360
On February 26, 1993, a horrific act of international terrorism struck the United States.

566
00:40:49,860 --> 00:40:55,980
In an attempt to bring down the Twin Towers, Islamic terrorists called the Fifth Liberation Army

567
00:40:55,981 --> 00:41:03,760
planted a bomb equivalent to 2,200 pounds of TNT in the parking garage of the second floor basement of Tower One

568
00:41:03,761 --> 00:41:05,220
at the World Trade Center.

569
00:41:05,221 --> 00:41:09,980
At 12.18 p.m., a huge explosion left six people dead

570
00:41:09,981 --> 00:41:11,620
and more than 1,000 injured.

571
00:41:13,400 --> 00:41:15,520
It was just a day that you don't forget.

572
00:41:15,900 --> 00:41:17,360
I wasn't injured at all in the bombing.

573
00:41:17,740 --> 00:41:19,900
I just was hit by a falling ceiling tile,

574
00:41:20,040 --> 00:41:21,220
and I guess it wasn't my time.

575
00:41:21,880 --> 00:41:24,680
I was about 150 feet from ground zero.

576
00:41:25,380 --> 00:41:27,640
I took the elevators down to the B-2 level,

577
00:41:27,720 --> 00:41:29,420
was walking down the hallway that day,

578
00:41:29,500 --> 00:41:32,240
and I just entered the door to my office,

579
00:41:32,380 --> 00:41:33,560
and the bomb went off.

580
00:41:33,561 --> 00:41:39,600
All I remember is a tremendous loud sound and a blast of wind going behind me.

581
00:41:39,680 --> 00:41:44,040
And the secretary that was walking with me that day, and she was heading down the hall to the carpenter's office.

582
00:41:44,540 --> 00:41:47,520
Both her legs were broken by this blast of wind.

583
00:41:47,600 --> 00:41:49,560
She wasn't hit by anything other than the blast force.

584
00:41:49,680 --> 00:41:54,320
Then, within about a half a second, my mind had realized that was a bomb.

585
00:41:54,380 --> 00:41:56,200
And that's what I yelled to everyone in the office.

586
00:41:56,340 --> 00:41:57,040
It's a bomb.

587
00:41:57,041 --> 00:41:57,741
Get out.

588
00:41:57,742 --> 00:42:01,200
The massive explosion ripped through the basement floor,

589
00:42:01,780 --> 00:42:05,040
creating a hole 100 feet wide, four stories deep,

590
00:42:05,480 --> 00:42:08,520
a tomb of crumpled cars, concrete, and twisted steel.

591
00:42:09,620 --> 00:42:12,900
However, the apocalyptic devastation hoped for by the terrorists

592
00:42:12,901 --> 00:42:14,560
did not come to pass.

593
00:42:15,300 --> 00:42:17,500
There was much damage caused by the explosion,

594
00:42:17,501 --> 00:42:23,160
but it was all in the surrounding area and very little inside of the footprint of the building,

595
00:42:23,460 --> 00:42:26,520
despite the fact that the bomb was placed immediately adjacent to it.

596
00:42:27,440 --> 00:42:33,660
Fortunately, the World Trade Center had been constructed to withstand hurricane winds of up to 150 miles per hour.

597
00:42:34,380 --> 00:42:38,440
The energy impact from those winds far exceeds the impact from the bombing.

598
00:42:39,500 --> 00:42:44,200
While the bomb was similar to the one that completely destroyed the Murrah Building in Oklahoma City,

599
00:42:45,020 --> 00:42:48,840
the steel vertical columns of the World Trade Center did not flinch.

600
00:42:48,841 --> 00:42:59,580
An analogy that one might put to that is if you take a firecracker and you put the firecracker on the ground and you put a can over it.

601
00:42:59,720 --> 00:43:04,180
Anyone who's done that realizes that the can goes shooting up in the air and the ground doesn't move.

602
00:43:04,320 --> 00:43:06,940
Well, the building was analogous to the ground.

603
00:43:07,120 --> 00:43:08,360
It didn't really move.

604
00:43:08,460 --> 00:43:12,400
That wall was very strong relative to anything else around.

605
00:43:12,401 --> 00:43:18,200
And so the force of the explosion, the shockwave, basically hit that surface and reflected.

606
00:43:18,840 --> 00:43:25,260
All that it caused was a crack in one of the support columns that you could stick a dollar bill through on one side.

607
00:43:25,261 --> 00:43:32,020
The other three sides of the column remained intact, and the loads just transferred to all the other structural steel in the complex.

608
00:43:34,420 --> 00:43:42,820
What concerned engineers most was the bathtub-like seven-story basement of the Trade Center complex that keeps out the waters of New York Harbor.

609
00:43:43,760 --> 00:43:48,840
The horizontal basement floors that formed the lateral support for the bathtubs had been destroyed.

610
00:43:50,420 --> 00:43:54,920
The loss of the basement floors meant that the support of the bathtub walls was in question.

611
00:43:55,720 --> 00:43:58,160
The entire wind load is carried to these columns.

612
00:43:59,220 --> 00:44:02,600
New York's weather forecast presented an even greater sense of urgency.

613
00:44:03,200 --> 00:44:07,020
There was concern, particularly since there was a windstorm predicted for the following week,

614
00:44:07,100 --> 00:44:12,040
that some of these columns might in fact get some additional load to the windstorm and would buckle.

615
00:44:12,041 --> 00:44:14,100
Columns can be like rulers.

616
00:44:14,101 --> 00:44:18,260
If you push on them enough, they'll just buckle out to the side at a given point.

617
00:44:19,120 --> 00:44:25,420
Within 36 hours, the damaged structural column and remaining columns were given solid additional bracing.

618
00:44:26,340 --> 00:44:28,660
Cleaning the extensive debris was another priority.

619
00:44:29,720 --> 00:44:34,660
It was a massive, almost like Normandy or D-Day.

620
00:44:34,960 --> 00:44:37,640
The cleanup operation was on a heroic scale.

621
00:44:37,940 --> 00:44:40,660
The engineering reconstruction was on a heroic scale.

622
00:44:42,040 --> 00:44:46,680
Working round the clock, an army of engineers, maintenance people and

623
00:44:46,681 --> 00:44:52,480
construction workers removed over 6,000 tons of debris. Final cleanup and repair

624
00:44:52,481 --> 00:44:58,800
costs reached more than 500 million dollars. 20 days after the blast, the

625
00:44:58,801 --> 00:45:03,160
World Trade Center reopened its doors. The first tenant to move back into his

626
00:45:03,161 --> 00:45:08,080
office was New York Governor Mario Cuomo. It's clean, it's comfortable, don't worry

627
00:45:08,081 --> 00:45:14,560
about it. New emergency communications were installed at key locations and

628
00:45:14,561 --> 00:45:19,480
increased security for truck deliveries and pickups were implemented. Tenants and

629
00:45:19,481 --> 00:45:23,380
visitors conducting business at the World Trade Center now wear electronic

630
00:45:23,381 --> 00:45:28,360
identification badges and access to the basement parking lots is restricted to

631
00:45:28,361 --> 00:45:30,960
tenants only.

632
00:45:30,961 --> 00:45:38,960
There was a loss of life.

633
00:45:38,961 --> 00:45:40,960
Some of my friends were killed in this incident.

634
00:45:40,961 --> 00:45:44,960
But it shows the tremendous resiliency and strength of these two towers,

635
00:45:44,961 --> 00:45:52,960
that such a significant event resulted in what was amazingly not that much property damage.

636
00:45:52,961 --> 00:45:58,960
And what could have been a real tragedy, only six people were killed here,

637
00:45:58,961 --> 00:46:02,200
rather than the tens of thousands that the terrorists wanted to kill.

638
00:46:02,400 --> 00:46:05,820
It was a symbolic act more than a murderous act.

639
00:46:06,520 --> 00:46:11,100
The World Trade Center is a symbol, an icon, a representation,

640
00:46:11,960 --> 00:46:17,140
not just of the port of New York and New Jersey or of lower Manhattan or of Wall Street,

641
00:46:17,141 --> 00:46:22,340
but indeed of the capitalist system and, by extension, all of America.

642
00:46:23,100 --> 00:46:27,100
Now more than ever before, the World Trade Center could be seen as a symbol

643
00:46:27,101 --> 00:46:29,300
of New York's strength and resilience.

644
00:46:29,940 --> 00:46:31,820
Great engineering projects like this

645
00:46:31,821 --> 00:46:35,840
become another way of our continuing to tell a story

646
00:46:35,841 --> 00:46:37,160
about who and what we are.

647
00:46:38,360 --> 00:46:39,520
You know, that's one of the ways

648
00:46:39,521 --> 00:46:41,560
that civilization reproduces itself.

649
00:46:42,220 --> 00:46:44,700
And sometimes we have to write these things out real big

650
00:46:44,701 --> 00:46:47,980
and then look at all the small stuff that goes into them,

651
00:46:48,260 --> 00:46:49,900
floor by floor by floor.

652
00:46:54,260 --> 00:46:57,000
Of course, the World Trade Center is gone now,

653
00:46:57,100 --> 00:46:59,651
reduced to a massive pile of rubble.

654
00:46:59,652 --> 00:47:03,760
The bombing of 1993 dwarfed by the horror of September 11,

655
00:47:04,020 --> 00:47:11,060
2001, when two hijacked passenger jets were turned into terrorist weapons and flown head-on

656
00:47:11,061 --> 00:47:16,319
into the Twin Towers. It is yet to be decided what will go there in its place.

657
00:47:16,320 --> 00:47:17,600
Some are suggesting

658
00:47:17,601 --> 00:47:23,750
a similar structure, a new symbol, this time of perseverance and perhaps defiance.

659
00:47:23,751 --> 00:47:24,500
Others are

660
00:47:24,501 --> 00:47:29,720
talking about a memorial, a memorial to the thousands of innocent civilians who died on

661
00:47:29,721 --> 00:47:30,421
that day.

662
00:47:30,422 --> 00:47:35,460
But whatever is put in its place, there will always be a World Trade Center

663
00:47:35,461 --> 00:47:41,880
towering in the memory of every American.

664
00:47:54,500 --> 00:48:07,600
Since this show was produced, we had the opportunity to talk again with Professor Angus Gillespie

665
00:48:07,601 --> 00:48:11,380
about his reaction to the terrible tragedy of September 11th.

666
00:48:11,820 --> 00:48:14,000
That's coming up next, after the break.

667
00:48:14,001 --> 00:48:16,745
We are joined now by Dr.

668
00:48:16,746 --> 00:48:24,180
Angus Cress Gillespie, a professor of American Studies at Rutgers

669
00:48:24,181 --> 00:48:28,840
University and the author of The Twin Towers, The Life of New York City's World Trade Center.

670
00:48:29,000 --> 00:48:30,060
Thank you very much for joining us.

671
00:48:30,061 --> 00:48:30,761
My pleasure.

672
00:48:32,080 --> 00:48:36,360
Are you able to comprehend yet what happened to those buildings?

673
00:48:37,080 --> 00:48:38,138
No, I'm shocked.

674
00:48:38,139 --> 00:48:42,900
When I drove in to New York and I looked over there, they weren't there.

675
00:48:42,901 --> 00:48:47,100
And, of course, I've been reading the news for the last three weeks.

676
00:48:47,160 --> 00:48:51,840
I should know, but I'm still stunned by the absence.

677
00:48:52,420 --> 00:48:54,460
It's like a kid with his teeth knocked out.

678
00:48:55,680 --> 00:48:57,260
Why do you think they were targets?

679
00:48:58,340 --> 00:49:05,860
Well, I argue in my book that the Twin Towers are not just a pair of skyscrapers, but symbols.

680
00:49:06,420 --> 00:49:08,180
And it's not too hard to figure out why.

681
00:49:08,181 --> 00:49:15,860
because of their proximity to Wall Street, by inference that they're identified with American capitalism,

682
00:49:16,240 --> 00:49:20,380
American finance, and by implication, the entire American way of life.

683
00:49:20,960 --> 00:49:28,060
Isn't it interesting in a way they're called the World Trade Center, yet they never really were a center of world trade?

684
00:49:28,380 --> 00:49:33,000
It was the intention originally of the Port Authority to make it a center of world trade,

685
00:49:33,001 --> 00:49:43,840
And they made a good faith effort initially to recruit importers, exporters, freight forwarders, customs house brokers, steamship companies.

686
00:49:44,800 --> 00:49:51,100
But the scheme unraveled because the price of the rentals went up and up and up.

687
00:49:51,240 --> 00:49:58,860
And these mom and pop companies were sort of squeezed out and replaced by blue chip companies in the finance industry.

688
00:49:58,861 --> 00:50:02,860
All the more reason why they're identified with American finance and capitalism.

689
00:50:03,560 --> 00:50:08,960
I think it's no coincidence that the terrorists chose the symbol of American military might, the Pentagon,

690
00:50:09,280 --> 00:50:12,540
and the symbol of American financial might, the Twin Towers.

691
00:50:12,740 --> 00:50:15,340
They were perfect symbolic targets.

692
00:50:16,200 --> 00:50:25,740
Is it too early to say that the terrorist attack would be a pivotal mark in not only our culture but in our history?

693
00:50:25,741 --> 00:50:30,240
I've been thinking about that and I think that's a fair statement.

694
00:50:30,241 --> 00:50:34,500
I think that people are saying to each other, where were you when you learned that this

695
00:50:34,501 --> 00:50:35,500
happened?

696
00:50:35,501 --> 00:50:38,780
And I don't remember this since the assassination of John F. Kennedy.

697
00:50:38,781 --> 00:50:44,020
I mean, if this is something that really changes our world view, we will all of us remember

698
00:50:44,021 --> 00:50:47,360
where we were when we learned of this.

699
00:50:47,361 --> 00:50:55,180
It's so recent still and we're in the throes of still trying to understand exactly what

700
00:50:55,181 --> 00:50:56,456
it all means.

701
00:50:56,457 --> 00:51:03,820
And I'm not sure any of us really have a clear idea of what, especially the

702
00:51:03,821 --> 00:51:05,682
long-lasting effects are going to be.

703
00:51:05,683 --> 00:51:08,500
Well, we Americans, we like to think of ourselves as the

704
00:51:08,501 --> 00:51:13,750
good guys. And we're always shocked when we learn that somebody doesn't like us.

705
00:51:13,751 --> 00:51:14,800
And I think that

706
00:51:14,801 --> 00:51:23,760
going back to that symbolic component, that American capitalism and American financial life

707
00:51:23,761 --> 00:51:25,260
depends on a number of assumptions.

708
00:51:25,940 --> 00:51:29,440
The fluidity of capital, the mobility of labor,

709
00:51:30,120 --> 00:51:33,140
the kind of secular assumption that everything has its price

710
00:51:33,141 --> 00:51:34,360
and can be bought and sold.

711
00:51:34,361 --> 00:51:37,760
And I think these ideas that we take for granted

712
00:51:37,761 --> 00:51:40,680
are deeply offensive to much of the rest of the world.

713
00:51:41,520 --> 00:51:44,580
So much was anticipated when this building was built.

714
00:51:46,260 --> 00:51:49,360
150 mile an hour hurricane force winds,

715
00:51:49,880 --> 00:51:52,260
even as Frank DiMartini talked about.

716
00:51:52,261 --> 00:51:57,420
the crash of a 707 into the superstructure.

717
00:51:58,300 --> 00:52:04,380
Could anyone have possibly foretold or thought for a second

718
00:52:04,381 --> 00:52:10,160
that terrorists would use jumbo jets as weapons of mass destruction

719
00:52:10,161 --> 00:52:11,400
to attack these buildings?

720
00:52:12,000 --> 00:52:12,700
No, I don't think so.

721
00:52:12,701 --> 00:52:16,640
I remember specifically talking to Frank Demartini about this

722
00:52:16,641 --> 00:52:20,540
and other structural engineers, what were the stresses and strains.

723
00:52:20,541 --> 00:52:25,940
And indeed, they did build it to withstand the impact of a jetliner.

724
00:52:26,400 --> 00:52:29,280
Of course, what they had in mind was an accident.

725
00:52:29,920 --> 00:52:33,280
Nobody envisioned kamikaze jetliners.

726
00:52:33,580 --> 00:52:42,840
And nobody envisioned the degree of high concept planning that these would be West Coast bound planes with a full load of fuel,

727
00:52:43,000 --> 00:52:47,680
which I'm told have the equivalent of four tanker trucks worth of fuel.

728
00:52:47,681 --> 00:52:51,980
and no building, I think, can be built to withstand that.

729
00:52:52,480 --> 00:52:54,120
It's a whole new ballgame.

730
00:52:54,680 --> 00:52:58,660
There's so much sentiment right now about rebuilding the buildings.

731
00:52:59,700 --> 00:53:02,580
At this point in time, good idea or bad idea?

732
00:53:04,180 --> 00:53:08,440
Emotionally, I would love to see them rebuilt exactly as they were, tit for tat.

733
00:53:10,660 --> 00:53:12,280
Intellectually, I'm skeptical.

734
00:53:12,680 --> 00:53:14,880
There's a lot of good reasons not to do it.

735
00:53:15,080 --> 00:53:16,780
Dr. Gillespie, thanks for joining us.

736
00:53:16,860 --> 00:53:17,560
It's a pleasure.

737
00:53:17,680 --> 00:53:18,880
Good evening.

738
00:53:21,400 --> 00:53:27,200
Completed in 1974, the Twin Towers remain the tallest buildings in New York City.

739
00:53:28,040 --> 00:53:34,100
That's a venerable accomplishment, given the city's long history of erecting skyscrapers to rival those that came before.

740
00:53:34,920 --> 00:53:41,080
But neither the World Trade Center nor any other tall building would have been possible without two engineering developments.

741
00:53:42,040 --> 00:53:46,160
First, the elevator. You can't have a tall office building without the elevator.

742
00:53:46,161 --> 00:53:49,000
and the first commercial building that was built with an elevator

743
00:53:49,001 --> 00:53:52,020
was the Equitable Life Assurance Company's headquarters on Broadway,

744
00:53:52,400 --> 00:53:53,860
built in the late 1860s.

745
00:53:55,700 --> 00:54:00,000
The next major advance was the development of the steel skeleton frame,

746
00:54:00,520 --> 00:54:04,740
which occurs in 1888 when the Tower Building is built,

747
00:54:04,860 --> 00:54:07,960
which was probably the world's first building with a steel skeleton frame.

748
00:54:08,900 --> 00:54:11,140
With the steel skeleton in the elevator,

749
00:54:11,141 --> 00:54:14,900
the Manhattan skyline began an extraordinary transformation.

750
00:54:15,720 --> 00:54:20,120
Within only a few years after the tower building was completed in the late 1880s,

751
00:54:20,500 --> 00:54:26,460
you have enormous skyscrapers being built that were rising 20, 30 stories.

752
00:54:27,420 --> 00:54:32,180
These would not have been possible either if the economics hadn't changed,

753
00:54:32,181 --> 00:54:37,140
if banking and commerce and trade and the insurance businesses weren't growing.

754
00:54:37,960 --> 00:54:40,460
During the first decades of the 20th century,

755
00:54:41,040 --> 00:54:43,560
some of New York's most famous skyscrapers were built.

756
00:54:44,380 --> 00:54:46,740
In 1903, the Flatiron Fuller Building.

757
00:54:47,440 --> 00:54:50,060
In 1909, the Metropolitan Life Tower.

758
00:54:50,560 --> 00:54:52,840
And in 1913, the Woolworth Building.

759
00:54:53,640 --> 00:54:57,000
The next great growth of skyscrapers occurred in the 1920s.

760
00:54:57,740 --> 00:55:01,880
It culminated in 1930 and 31 with buildings like the Chrysler Building

761
00:55:01,881 --> 00:55:03,540
and, of course, the Empire State Building,

762
00:55:03,541 --> 00:55:08,320
which was the world's tallest building for the 40 years before the World Trade Center.

763
00:55:09,340 --> 00:55:13,300
During each one of those cycles, there was a boom in the economy

764
00:55:13,301 --> 00:55:16,180
that drove the heights of buildings ever taller.

765
00:55:17,260 --> 00:55:21,600
It was during the euphoric boom years immediately following World War II

766
00:55:21,601 --> 00:55:27,160
that New York lawmakers conceived the idea of an international or world trade center.

767
00:55:28,000 --> 00:55:30,800
They expected an increase in global trade

768
00:55:30,801 --> 00:55:34,640
and believed New York could be more competitive with other port cities

769
00:55:34,641 --> 00:55:38,960
if all businesses related to trade were housed under one roof.

770
00:55:38,961 --> 00:55:49,440
The idea came about that there would be a big trade mart based on the ancient fair in Leipzig, which went back to the Middle Ages.

771
00:55:49,560 --> 00:56:00,180
There would be a fair, a permanent trade exposition in New York where people from all over the world could come and show their goods.

772
00:56:01,080 --> 00:56:07,380
But when market research revealed that the city would benefit more by modernizing its ports, the idea was scrapped.

773
00:56:08,100 --> 00:56:11,000
It wouldn't resurface again for more than a decade,

774
00:56:11,600 --> 00:56:14,420
and only then, under the aegis of urban renewal

775
00:56:14,421 --> 00:56:17,460
and the influence of one of New York's most famous heirs,

776
00:56:18,320 --> 00:56:22,458
David Rockefeller, grandson of Standard Oil founder and billionaire John D.

777
00:56:22,459 --> 00:56:23,159
Rockefeller.

778
00:56:24,960 --> 00:56:30,120
In 1958, Rockefeller spearheaded what was called the Billion Dollar Plan

779
00:56:30,121 --> 00:56:33,240
to stimulate business and revitalize Lower Manhattan.

780
00:56:33,241 --> 00:56:41,240
His vision was to spend a billion dollars of private and public monies to create a kind of gleaming Futuropolis.

781
00:56:41,241 --> 00:56:50,240
The core of this thing is going to be this great public purpose, a world trade center that will secure the future of New York City's port forever.

782
00:56:50,241 --> 00:56:54,240
So they said, well, here's all this stuff we can demolish over on the east side.

783
00:56:54,241 --> 00:56:58,240
This, by the way, is where the South Street Seaport is today.

784
00:56:58,241 --> 00:57:00,240
All the preserved buildings are today.

785
00:57:00,241 --> 00:57:03,180
are today. But at that point, it was considered fair game.

786
00:57:03,181 --> 00:57:05,440
The 19th century buildings, knock them down.

787
00:57:05,441 --> 00:57:10,780
It should also be pointed out that David Rockefeller's brother was Nelson Rockefeller,

788
00:57:10,940 --> 00:57:12,530
who was the governor of New York.

789
00:57:12,531 --> 00:57:15,180
So this is very helpful in pushing the project through.

790
00:57:15,620 --> 00:57:21,700
In fact, some people have said that the Twin Towers should be renamed David and Nelson.

791
00:57:22,500 --> 00:57:27,840
To fund and build the new project, David Rockefeller and his supporters turned to the Port Authority,

792
00:57:28,300 --> 00:57:32,720
an agency chartered in 1921 by the states of New York and New Jersey

793
00:57:32,721 --> 00:57:36,280
to build and operate any terminal or transportation facility

794
00:57:36,281 --> 00:57:39,340
within a 25-mile radius of the Statue of Liberty.

795
00:57:41,640 --> 00:57:43,880
The Port Authority is a hybrid creature,

796
00:57:44,360 --> 00:57:48,800
partly a government agency and partly operating as a private business.

797
00:57:48,801 --> 00:57:54,640
No one else had quite the same combination of deep pockets and financial resources,

798
00:57:54,641 --> 00:58:00,360
the right of eminent domain, and the technical engineering skills to launch a big project.

799
00:58:01,420 --> 00:58:08,320
The other great power that the Port Authority has stems from the nature of public authorities in general.

800
00:58:08,620 --> 00:58:13,220
If you want to build a project that costs a billion dollars, you simply sell bonds to do that,

801
00:58:13,260 --> 00:58:17,760
and then you charge rents or tolls or something like that to pay those bonds off.

802
00:58:18,340 --> 00:58:22,880
The self-supporting agency built, owned, and operated several megastructures,

803
00:58:23,680 --> 00:58:26,360
such as the Lincoln Tunnel and the George Washington Bridge.

804
00:58:26,860 --> 00:58:31,080
By the early 1960s, the World Trade Center project was added to its portfolio

805
00:58:31,081 --> 00:58:34,540
under the auspices of Port Authority director Austin J. Tobin.

806
00:58:35,240 --> 00:58:40,540
There was a kind of professionalism that the Port Authority was known for

807
00:58:40,541 --> 00:58:44,100
in its engineering and its planning and its whole way of carrying things out,

808
00:58:44,340 --> 00:58:47,440
which was really impeccable, really impeccable.

809
00:58:47,540 --> 00:58:51,740
And Tobin was the guy who did that, and he was the central point of control.

810
00:58:51,741 --> 00:58:52,980
He was the Sun King.

811
00:58:54,300 --> 00:58:56,760
It was decided, okay, it's going to be a large project.

812
00:58:56,880 --> 00:58:58,260
It's going to be done by the Port Authority.

813
00:58:58,540 --> 00:59:00,760
It's going to be concerned with world trade.

814
00:59:01,040 --> 00:59:02,000
So far, so good.

815
00:59:02,320 --> 00:59:07,780
The original plan was to put it on the east side of lower Manhattan.

816
00:59:08,460 --> 00:59:12,420
Bear in mind that the Port Authority is a bi-state agency.

817
00:59:12,860 --> 00:59:16,260
So understandably, the governor of New Jersey was concerned.

818
00:59:16,360 --> 00:59:19,660
He said, well, you're building this World Trade Center for New York.

819
00:59:19,661 --> 00:59:21,020
What are you going to do for us?

820
00:59:21,740 --> 00:59:26,980
The Port Authority agreed to take over New Jersey's bankrupt Hudson and Manhattan Commuter Railroad,

821
00:59:27,340 --> 00:59:30,240
now known as PATH for Port Authority Trans-Hudson.

822
00:59:30,880 --> 00:59:35,660
The railroad had been running since 1908 and was in dire need of a facelift.

823
00:59:36,500 --> 00:59:39,420
Its terminus was on the west side of Lower Manhattan.

824
00:59:40,100 --> 00:59:46,320
Once this deal was in place, that New Jersey was getting the PATH, New York was getting the Trade Center,

825
00:59:47,100 --> 00:59:49,320
gradually it hit everyone.

826
00:59:49,321 --> 00:59:51,560
Why not put it on the west side?

827
00:59:51,561 --> 00:59:53,960
And then he could do two projects at once.

828
00:59:53,961 --> 00:59:56,160
It was a brilliant breakthrough.

829
00:59:56,161 --> 00:59:58,360
And the governor of New Jersey said,

830
00:59:58,361 --> 01:00:00,560
at least we can see the damn thing.

831
01:00:00,561 --> 01:00:03,640
The World Trade Center project was moved from the east side

832
01:00:03,641 --> 01:00:06,840
to the middle of a neighborhood known as Radio Row.

833
01:00:06,841 --> 01:00:09,480
This was the center of the radio industry,

834
01:00:09,481 --> 01:00:12,600
where I remember going down there as a child with my father

835
01:00:12,601 --> 01:00:14,280
to buy radio tubes.

836
01:00:14,281 --> 01:00:21,120
It was a sort of a bazaar of retailers,

837
01:00:21,700 --> 01:00:24,960
mostly involving consumer electronics of some kind.

838
01:00:25,580 --> 01:00:31,080
And this was where the first TVs were sold off the shelves in the United States.

839
01:00:31,180 --> 01:00:33,740
I mean, this was the consumer appliance mecca.

840
01:00:35,700 --> 01:00:37,960
Radio Row merchants protested,

841
01:00:38,280 --> 01:00:40,960
but their shops would vanish by way of the wrecking ball

842
01:00:40,961 --> 01:00:44,380
as plans to build the biggest complex in Lower Manhattan geared up.

843
01:00:44,840 --> 01:00:48,960
Ultimately, they didn't have much of an argument

844
01:00:48,961 --> 01:00:54,740
when you compared what, in the minds of the various judges

845
01:00:54,741 --> 01:00:56,900
who heard the cases, was the public good.

846
01:00:57,600 --> 01:00:58,780
Well, I mean, you're just a business.

847
01:00:58,880 --> 01:01:00,700
You can go and you can move someplace else.

848
01:01:01,260 --> 01:01:02,840
But this is Lower Manhattan,

849
01:01:03,160 --> 01:01:06,400
and the financial district has to expand.

850
01:01:06,620 --> 01:01:08,640
I mean, look, you guys got to get out of the way.

851
01:01:09,640 --> 01:01:11,420
The merchants were bought out.

852
01:01:12,240 --> 01:01:16,040
Planners decided the World Trade Center site would occupy 16 acres,

853
01:01:16,320 --> 01:01:18,760
but it was not yet known who would design it.

854
01:01:19,360 --> 01:01:22,740
When Yammer received the letter, we looked at the letter,

855
01:01:23,300 --> 01:01:25,560
and Yammer kind of called us together and said,

856
01:01:26,060 --> 01:01:28,060
you know, it just shows how careful you've got to be

857
01:01:28,061 --> 01:01:29,880
when you write a letter of invitation like this,

858
01:01:29,940 --> 01:01:33,400
because here's a project that obviously has a misplaced decimal point.

859
01:01:33,401 --> 01:01:34,800
It's got too many zeros.

860
01:01:34,801 --> 01:01:38,680
who would be asking our firm to do a project of $250 million.

861
01:01:39,080 --> 01:01:41,520
But we prevailed upon Yamaha to follow through and make a call.

862
01:01:42,240 --> 01:01:44,500
It was the call that changed his life.

863
01:01:45,320 --> 01:01:48,040
Out of a field of more than a dozen prominent architects,

864
01:01:48,660 --> 01:01:53,460
Austin Tobin and the Port Authority chose Minoru Yamasaki and his firm out of Michigan

865
01:01:53,461 --> 01:01:56,560
to design one of the most important buildings in the world.

866
01:01:57,340 --> 01:01:59,320
The year was 1962.

867
01:02:01,100 --> 01:02:04,600
Yamasaki was a rather strange choice as an architect.

868
01:02:04,800 --> 01:02:07,840
because he had never built anything on this scale before,

869
01:02:08,040 --> 01:02:13,900
and most of his previous buildings had been rather precious boxes,

870
01:02:14,500 --> 01:02:19,200
buildings that were detailed and ornamented in a way

871
01:02:19,201 --> 01:02:23,220
that you wouldn't expect to relate to the size of the World Trade Center.

872
01:02:23,221 --> 01:02:29,500
He was also famously a strange pick because he had fear of heights.

873
01:02:30,620 --> 01:02:33,720
What impressed the Port Authority was Yamasaki's grasp of engineering

874
01:02:33,721 --> 01:02:34,840
and his character.

875
01:02:35,460 --> 01:02:36,960
He was a self-made man,

876
01:02:37,360 --> 01:02:39,660
rising from a hardscrabble childhood in Seattle

877
01:02:39,661 --> 01:02:41,900
to acclaim as an award-winning architect.

878
01:02:42,480 --> 01:02:45,580
They saw someone who was similar in some ways

879
01:02:45,581 --> 01:02:47,040
to them and to their values.

880
01:02:47,460 --> 01:02:49,680
This was not an architectural prima donna.

881
01:02:50,540 --> 01:02:52,400
Yamasaki and his team went to work

882
01:02:52,401 --> 01:02:53,460
studying and designing

883
01:02:53,461 --> 01:02:55,600
over a hundred different schemes and models.

884
01:02:56,600 --> 01:03:01,300
There were times when something would just simply go wrong.

885
01:03:01,301 --> 01:03:07,940
And Yamaha would look at it and would see the flaws in it quickly and very compassionately say, let's try a little something else.

886
01:03:09,140 --> 01:03:14,120
Yamasaki finally settled on a two-tower development between 80 and 90 stories tall.

887
01:03:15,120 --> 01:03:18,360
Its plaza was evocative of the Piazza San Marco in Venice.

888
01:03:18,800 --> 01:03:27,520
Light poured into interior public areas, and windows were designed shoulder-width to give people a sense of security, no matter how high the floor.

889
01:03:27,521 --> 01:03:31,600
But the Port Authority wanted to make one important change.

890
01:03:32,300 --> 01:03:36,520
Given the square footage demands of the World Trade Center project,

891
01:03:36,760 --> 01:03:38,760
the Public Relations Department began to say,

892
01:03:38,880 --> 01:03:42,360
well, why not make it the tallest building in the world?

893
01:03:43,280 --> 01:03:48,740
He was very, very nervous about that because of the cost of pushing those extra floors in.

894
01:03:48,741 --> 01:03:54,040
He was fearful that the economics of the project would dictate to stripping away

895
01:03:54,041 --> 01:03:58,200
some of the things at the ground level for the planting in the plaza,

896
01:03:58,340 --> 01:04:01,160
the flowers in the plaza, the sculpture in the plaza,

897
01:04:01,660 --> 01:04:05,500
and even in the lobbies, the amount of marble that's in the lobbies,

898
01:04:05,620 --> 01:04:09,240
the stainless steel doors, all of these things that he was so fond of.

899
01:04:09,520 --> 01:04:10,860
The budget was increased.

900
01:04:11,260 --> 01:04:13,020
Little, if anything, would be scaled back.

901
01:04:13,500 --> 01:04:16,580
And the famous 1,250-foot Empire State Building,

902
01:04:16,880 --> 01:04:19,500
the tallest building in the world since 1931,

903
01:04:19,501 --> 01:04:23,860
was about to be eclipsed by the new 1,360-foot towers

904
01:04:23,861 --> 01:04:25,140
of the World Trade Center.

905
01:04:25,760 --> 01:04:29,840
The World Trade Center was conceived back in the 1960s

906
01:04:29,841 --> 01:04:35,520
when I think American ambition and a faith in technology

907
01:04:35,521 --> 01:04:37,300
was really at its peak.

908
01:04:39,300 --> 01:04:43,300
If you think of the other sort of supersized topics

909
01:04:43,301 --> 01:04:45,540
of the day, like jumbo jets,

910
01:04:46,240 --> 01:04:50,580
you have some sense of the sort of ambitious love of bigness

911
01:04:50,581 --> 01:04:52,480
that the World Trade Center represents.

912
01:04:53,860 --> 01:05:03,160
But to achieve that bigness, it would take engineering innovations and an army of workers manning revolutionary equipment.

913
01:05:05,380 --> 01:05:11,640
Since it opened in 1931, the Empire State Building had held the title of the world's tallest building.

914
01:05:12,400 --> 01:05:17,260
Now, over 35 years later, the formidable task presented to engineers and architects

915
01:05:17,261 --> 01:05:22,840
was how to build a structure 100 feet taller than the Empire State Building and do it twice.

916
01:05:23,860 --> 01:05:27,280
This massive undertaking presented a number of unique and complex challenges.

917
01:05:28,160 --> 01:05:30,740
First, there was the matter of the building site itself.

918
01:05:31,500 --> 01:05:34,440
A major problem in the construction of the World Trade Center

919
01:05:34,441 --> 01:05:37,060
was the fact it was going to be built in Lower Manhattan

920
01:05:37,061 --> 01:05:40,660
in an area which was fundamentally filled.

921
01:05:40,661 --> 01:05:43,340
You have to think about the geology of the area.

922
01:05:43,780 --> 01:05:48,620
Lower Manhattan represents the confluence of two large rivers and the ocean.

923
01:05:49,440 --> 01:05:54,900
Since the 1600s, the waterfront had been extended by over 700 feet by landfill operations.

924
01:05:55,680 --> 01:05:59,280
It had been filled with dirt, rubble, old piers and wharves.

925
01:06:00,140 --> 01:06:03,920
To reach bedrock, engineers would have to dig down 65 feet

926
01:06:03,921 --> 01:06:07,960
and at the same time not disturb the foundations of the surrounding buildings.

927
01:06:08,320 --> 01:06:13,840
If you start to dig a hole, what happens is that if you only go down 3 feet,

928
01:06:14,240 --> 01:06:16,180
your hole fills up with water.

929
01:06:16,181 --> 01:06:21,540
They needed to dig not just a three-foot hall, but a very, very large hall.

930
01:06:21,920 --> 01:06:25,900
It would have been the equivalent of pumping out the entire Hudson River.

931
01:06:26,560 --> 01:06:33,300
Not only that, even if you were able to pump all the water out, which is virtually impossible,

932
01:06:33,880 --> 01:06:35,960
the surrounding buildings would have collapsed.

933
01:06:37,180 --> 01:06:40,880
The first obstacle, then, was how to control and remove the water

934
01:06:40,881 --> 01:06:43,380
while laying the foundation of the enormous structures.

935
01:06:43,381 --> 01:06:51,380
They had to come up with extremely unique and wonderful engineering techniques to actually

936
01:06:51,381 --> 01:06:58,100
pour that foundation while all this water was pressing in on the foundation structure

937
01:06:58,101 --> 01:06:58,801
itself.

938
01:06:59,600 --> 01:07:04,860
The solution came from engineer Ray Monte, who would utilize the slurry trench method.

939
01:07:05,500 --> 01:07:09,880
It had been used for subway construction in Europe and in Canada, and now would be used

940
01:07:09,881 --> 01:07:11,820
for the first time in the United States.

941
01:07:11,821 --> 01:07:18,080
For this project, excavating machines would dig a three-foot-wide trench down to bedrock.

942
01:07:18,680 --> 01:07:21,820
As dirt and rock were removed, they would be replaced by the slurry.

943
01:07:23,080 --> 01:07:29,860
The slurry is a mixture of water and bentonite, a type of expansive gray clay that has a swelling tendency

944
01:07:29,861 --> 01:07:33,920
and would plug any holes along the side as excavators dug deeper.

945
01:07:35,880 --> 01:07:38,820
The trench was dug in 22-foot segments.

946
01:07:38,821 --> 01:07:43,820
Then the workers lowered into each slurry-filled segment a pre-assembled

947
01:07:43,821 --> 01:07:50,820
seven-story high steel cage of reinforcing rods weighing 65 tons.

948
01:07:50,821 --> 01:07:55,820
The cage provided a framework for the concrete wall that was to come.

949
01:07:55,821 --> 01:08:01,820
The concrete would be poured in at the top of the trench through a pipe that resembled an elephant's trunk.

950
01:08:01,821 --> 01:08:07,820
The concrete was heavier than the clay, so it sank to the bottom and the clay was rose up

951
01:08:07,821 --> 01:08:11,120
and it was reclaimed and used for the next section of trenching.

952
01:08:11,860 --> 01:08:17,720
All in all, construction workers had to make 152 of those 22-foot trench segments

953
01:08:17,721 --> 01:08:21,580
to enclose an area two blocks wide and four blocks long.

954
01:08:22,320 --> 01:08:26,460
It was like building a bathtub, only it's a bathtub in reverse.

955
01:08:27,060 --> 01:08:32,340
The purpose of this bathtub, 800 feet long, 200 feet wide, 70 feet deep,

956
01:08:32,640 --> 01:08:35,860
was not to contain water, but to keep water out.

957
01:08:35,861 --> 01:08:41,300
And the bathtub, you could have swum, Moby Dick could have done laps in the bathtub because it was that big.

958
01:08:41,860 --> 01:08:45,800
A unique method and used extensively now.

959
01:08:45,900 --> 01:08:50,060
In fact, a number of people from other cities come to look at how the Trade Center was built,

960
01:08:50,280 --> 01:08:52,940
for example in Denver, and are using the same method.

961
01:08:54,280 --> 01:08:56,760
The site posed another major challenge.

962
01:08:57,500 --> 01:09:02,380
The tunnels containing the path commuter rail lines lay underground within the excavation area.

963
01:09:02,381 --> 01:09:07,380
Over 130,000 passengers traveled daily on these lines.

964
01:09:07,381 --> 01:09:13,380
They had to dig around these extremely ancient cast iron tunnels for the Hudson tubes,

965
01:09:13,381 --> 01:09:18,380
what is now the PATH, Port Authority Trans-Hudson commuter line.

966
01:09:18,381 --> 01:09:26,380
And these were really, really old, fragile, literally tubes in which these trains ran.

967
01:09:26,381 --> 01:09:31,880
And to do the foundation work required almost micro-surgery

968
01:09:31,881 --> 01:09:36,380
to excavate all around these and support them

969
01:09:36,381 --> 01:09:38,600
while they were doing all the foundation work

970
01:09:38,601 --> 01:09:40,240
and then raising the building over it.

971
01:09:40,820 --> 01:09:45,380
The fragile tubes were supported in protective cradles 40 feet in diameter

972
01:09:45,381 --> 01:09:47,680
while excavation continued around them.

973
01:09:48,220 --> 01:09:51,580
We had to protect them over about four years

974
01:09:51,581 --> 01:09:56,340
while we were building from scratch to completion.

975
01:09:56,380 --> 01:09:58,840
all the time, kept them running,

976
01:09:59,000 --> 01:10:01,560
never created inconvenience for the passengers.

977
01:10:03,500 --> 01:10:05,360
Once excavation was complete,

978
01:10:05,880 --> 01:10:08,320
there was the burden of moving the excavated landfill.

979
01:10:09,080 --> 01:10:11,040
To remove the million cubic yards

980
01:10:11,041 --> 01:10:13,480
or 100,000 truckloads worth of landfill,

981
01:10:14,140 --> 01:10:15,960
the solution by Austin Tobin

982
01:10:15,961 --> 01:10:18,240
seemed to be a win-win situation.

983
01:10:20,620 --> 01:10:23,480
Instead of carting this dirt all the way to New Jersey

984
01:10:23,481 --> 01:10:24,860
or hauling it way out to sea,

985
01:10:24,861 --> 01:10:27,780
simply place it on the west side of West Street,

986
01:10:28,140 --> 01:10:29,860
but not just to dump it in the river,

987
01:10:30,280 --> 01:10:33,800
but to carefully contain it within a series of coffer dams,

988
01:10:33,801 --> 01:10:37,340
such as, in effect, you would be increasing the size of New York City.

989
01:10:37,720 --> 01:10:39,200
And that's precisely what they did.

990
01:10:39,820 --> 01:10:43,580
They measured out an area just west of West Street,

991
01:10:43,820 --> 01:10:47,300
which would extend 700 feet out into the Hudson River,

992
01:10:47,800 --> 01:10:49,600
and be 1,400 feet long,

993
01:10:49,601 --> 01:10:53,060
in other words, about the size of six city blocks.

994
01:10:53,700 --> 01:10:57,180
They built a drum-shaped box of cellular cofferdams,

995
01:10:57,620 --> 01:11:01,640
a watertight enclosure to hold the material excavated from the building site.

996
01:11:02,080 --> 01:11:07,620
These were really like big sunken cylinders, each 63 feet in diameter,

997
01:11:08,100 --> 01:11:11,900
each one filled with 6,000 cubic yards of sand.

998
01:11:11,901 --> 01:11:18,660
So you would create this barrier 700 feet out to the river and 1,400 feet long,

999
01:11:18,661 --> 01:11:22,280
behind which you would put the excavated fill

1000
01:11:22,281 --> 01:11:26,180
and thus create 23 acres of new land.

1001
01:11:26,420 --> 01:11:28,520
As a result of the excavated material,

1002
01:11:29,060 --> 01:11:32,600
the landfill created some of the most valuable real estate in the country.

1003
01:11:33,920 --> 01:11:36,640
New York got $90 million worth of land

1004
01:11:36,641 --> 01:11:41,000
that would eventually become Battery Park and the World Financial Center.

1005
01:11:42,980 --> 01:11:45,180
Although the foundations were in place,

1006
01:11:45,181 --> 01:11:50,300
there was still the problem of bringing thousands of tons of steel up over a hundred floors.

1007
01:11:51,260 --> 01:11:54,732
In past construction of skyscrapers, derricks were used.

1008
01:11:54,733 --> 01:11:56,840
But a derrick had to be constantly

1009
01:11:56,841 --> 01:12:02,382
lifted to a higher position, one floor at a time, costing several man hours.

1010
01:12:02,383 --> 01:12:03,840
Derricks also had to

1011
01:12:03,841 --> 01:12:08,620
be powered from the ground, with extremely long cables running down through the floor already

1012
01:12:08,621 --> 01:12:14,620
built? The answer was a self-powered crane that came from across the globe.

1013
01:12:14,621 --> 01:12:20,620
These were the so-called kangaroo cranes, nicknamed because they're coming from Australia,

1014
01:12:20,621 --> 01:12:23,851
and they were put at the corner of each building.

1015
01:12:23,852 --> 01:12:26,620
And what these kangaroo cranes essentially

1016
01:12:26,621 --> 01:12:34,620
were able to do was to hoist themselves up with automatic machinery built into the crane,

1017
01:12:34,621 --> 01:12:37,940
such that the crane could rise as the building went up.

1018
01:12:38,820 --> 01:12:41,360
The crane's diesel motors supplied the power,

1019
01:12:41,520 --> 01:12:45,220
driving a series of hydraulic lifts installed at the base of each corner.

1020
01:12:50,180 --> 01:12:53,980
And an interesting problem at the very end is how do you get the kangaroo crane down?

1021
01:12:53,981 --> 01:12:58,820
And the way that was done is they had to be disassembled and brought down by elevator.

1022
01:13:00,020 --> 01:13:03,560
The cranes would raise a structure unlike any in the world.

1023
01:13:04,320 --> 01:13:05,940
Before the buildings would be finished,

1024
01:13:06,200 --> 01:13:08,880
more radical construction and engineering methods

1025
01:13:08,881 --> 01:13:11,540
would forever change how skyscrapers were built.

1026
01:13:13,900 --> 01:13:17,980
Construction of the Twin Towers began in August 1968.

1027
01:13:19,160 --> 01:13:22,840
Architect Minoru Yamasaki would move away from conventional techniques

1028
01:13:22,841 --> 01:13:24,880
with a completely different type of skeleton.

1029
01:13:25,400 --> 01:13:29,100
The common sense way of understanding the construction of a skyscraper,

1030
01:13:29,101 --> 01:13:34,258
we think back to our childhood as building it like an erector set.

1031
01:13:34,259 --> 01:13:36,680
You have little pieces of steel

1032
01:13:36,681 --> 01:13:42,362
which you hold together with rivets and grommets, and it's like building a cage.

1033
01:13:42,363 --> 01:13:43,640
This is the normal

1034
01:13:43,641 --> 01:13:48,296
way of building a steel skyscraper. It's a cage-like construction.

1035
01:13:48,297 --> 01:13:50,060
You have vertical members

1036
01:13:50,061 --> 01:13:56,380
and horizontal members, and you build little boxes, and you pile box upon box upon box,

1037
01:13:56,381 --> 01:13:58,100
and thus you have a skyscraper.

1038
01:13:59,100 --> 01:14:02,340
Yamasaki wanted something different.

1039
01:14:02,341 --> 01:14:06,800
What he decided on was a revolutionary way of constructing high-rises,

1040
01:14:07,560 --> 01:14:09,300
the exterior skeleton system.

1041
01:14:10,180 --> 01:14:15,260
What Yamasaki came up with, in conjunction with structural engineers,

1042
01:14:15,700 --> 01:14:20,560
is to make the skin of the building much stronger

1043
01:14:20,561 --> 01:14:23,860
so that you wouldn't need internal columns.

1044
01:14:24,860 --> 01:14:30,920
Ever since the 19th century, skyscrapers have been built with a network of interior-supporting columns.

1045
01:14:32,080 --> 01:14:36,880
Their exterior walls were merely curtain walls that emitted light and kept out the rain.

1046
01:14:37,640 --> 01:14:42,040
But in the World Trade Center, its tough exterior walls would carry the vertical loads

1047
01:14:42,041 --> 01:14:45,820
and have the strength to resist the enormous force of lateral winds as well.

1048
01:14:46,480 --> 01:14:50,760
High-strength steel that had not been available before made this possible.

1049
01:14:50,761 --> 01:14:58,900
A good analogy, I think, to explain it is the World Trade Center towers are like stalks of celery.

1050
01:14:59,260 --> 01:15:02,540
The rigidity comes from the outside, not from the inside.

1051
01:15:03,020 --> 01:15:05,420
And this also accounts for their unique appearance.

1052
01:15:07,540 --> 01:15:13,980
The building was designed to have a fully loaded 707 crash into it.

1053
01:15:14,380 --> 01:15:17,140
That was the largest plane at the time.

1054
01:15:17,141 --> 01:15:24,140
I believe that the building probably could sustain multiple impacts of jetliners

1055
01:15:24,141 --> 01:15:32,140
because this structure is like the mosquito netting on your screen door,

1056
01:15:32,141 --> 01:15:40,140
this intense grid, and the jet plane is just a pencil puncturing that screen netting.

1057
01:15:40,141 --> 01:15:43,140
It really does nothing to the screen netting.

1058
01:15:43,141 --> 01:15:47,920
To achieve this kind of strength, engineers conducted two comprehensive tests.

1059
01:15:48,720 --> 01:15:51,380
The first was a boundary layer wind tunnel test.

1060
01:15:52,220 --> 01:15:57,120
Scale models of the World Trade Center, along with a large section of the city, were put into a wind tunnel.

1061
01:15:58,160 --> 01:16:03,120
Planners spent hours doing mathematical stress calculations of various wind effects on the building.

1062
01:16:03,640 --> 01:16:07,720
With the World Trade Center, it was the first time that for a high-rise building

1063
01:16:07,721 --> 01:16:12,540
that comprehensive boundary layer wind tunnel studies had been performed.

1064
01:16:13,140 --> 01:16:18,840
And previous to this, people had studied the effects of wind for aeronautical projects,

1065
01:16:19,120 --> 01:16:24,220
for use in cars, designing cars, but for buildings that had not been used.

1066
01:16:24,760 --> 01:16:29,940
Thirty years later, that type of testing is standard practice in the structural engineering industry.

1067
01:16:31,760 --> 01:16:34,740
The second was a human sensitivity test.

1068
01:16:34,980 --> 01:16:37,240
They created a very interesting experiment.

1069
01:16:38,000 --> 01:16:42,980
The bait for this particular experiment is they were offering free eye examinations.

1070
01:16:43,140 --> 01:16:46,320
and unbeknownst to the people while they were getting their eye exam,

1071
01:16:46,400 --> 01:16:47,340
it was a legitimate deal.

1072
01:16:47,380 --> 01:16:49,480
They did get the eye exam, but what they didn't know

1073
01:16:49,481 --> 01:16:53,580
was that the room in which the eye examination was taking place

1074
01:16:53,581 --> 01:16:57,440
was being subjected to variable forces of sway.

1075
01:16:58,160 --> 01:16:59,680
And what they were trying to determine is

1076
01:16:59,681 --> 01:17:03,080
how much sway would the average person tolerate

1077
01:17:03,081 --> 01:17:05,440
before he or she started to object.

1078
01:17:05,980 --> 01:17:08,860
Amazingly, people tolerated sway equivalent to that

1079
01:17:08,861 --> 01:17:11,340
caused by 150 mile-per-hour winds.

1080
01:17:11,341 --> 01:17:19,840
The exterior skeleton system also allowed for a maximum of open, usable, and rentable column-free floor space.

1081
01:17:20,580 --> 01:17:23,260
This helped create another engineering first.

1082
01:17:24,220 --> 01:17:28,920
Two buildings, each 110 stories high, could have produced a maze of elevators.

1083
01:17:29,820 --> 01:17:35,680
Tobin and Yamasaki avoided that problem by borrowing a key concept from New York's famed subway system.

1084
01:17:36,340 --> 01:17:40,700
They proposed dividing each tower building into three parts or zones.

1085
01:17:41,340 --> 01:17:48,340
Each zone would have its own lobby, and people would be able to transfer from express to local in the second and third zones.

1086
01:17:49,360 --> 01:17:51,760
Initially, the idea was almost shot down.

1087
01:17:52,380 --> 01:17:59,880
Austin Tobin, who was the executive director of the Port Authority, had a blue-ribbon panel of architectural experts.

1088
01:18:00,760 --> 01:18:09,840
And the blue-ribbon panel unanimously said that the tenants would never accept such an arrangement that had never been done before,

1089
01:18:09,841 --> 01:18:14,720
and that people would not tolerate the waste of time

1090
01:18:14,721 --> 01:18:18,260
of taking two separate elevators to reach their destination.

1091
01:18:19,400 --> 01:18:21,620
But Tobin continued to move forward

1092
01:18:21,621 --> 01:18:25,400
and even asked Otis Elevator to build pioneering elevators

1093
01:18:25,401 --> 01:18:28,260
with sliding doors in both the front and the rear.

1094
01:18:28,960 --> 01:18:32,060
The first people in the elevator were also the first people out.

1095
01:18:32,061 --> 01:18:35,000
As it turns out, people readily accepted it

1096
01:18:35,001 --> 01:18:38,280
because the truth was that this system,

1097
01:18:38,281 --> 01:18:41,300
even though it involved two rides, the express ride and the local ride,

1098
01:18:41,560 --> 01:18:45,180
actually saved time and resulted in a shorter wait.

1099
01:18:45,540 --> 01:18:49,000
We're essentially three buildings stacked one on top of another.

1100
01:18:49,580 --> 01:18:53,680
A unique concept now used in numerous other super high-rise buildings around the world,

1101
01:18:53,960 --> 01:18:57,680
whether it be Shanghai or Malaysia, but very innovative at its time.

1102
01:18:58,340 --> 01:19:00,160
During the building of the World Trade Center,

1103
01:19:00,460 --> 01:19:05,600
each problem faced by engineers and contractors resulted in a revolutionary solution.

1104
01:19:05,601 --> 01:19:11,140
This building is not just a machine, it's an intelligent machine or a smart machine.

1105
01:19:11,860 --> 01:19:17,420
And one of the things that, you know, again, the Port Authority was way out ahead of everybody else

1106
01:19:17,421 --> 01:19:22,660
in really marshalling the best, not just structural engineering minds,

1107
01:19:22,740 --> 01:19:25,700
but also the best electrical and electronic engineering minds.

1108
01:19:27,100 --> 01:19:33,220
All of these pioneering ideas created a complex that was structurally sound and incredibly efficient.

1109
01:19:33,221 --> 01:19:39,680
Its strength in its very existence would be most severely tested not by high winds or a jet airplane,

1110
01:19:40,360 --> 01:19:43,480
but by an unforeseen and appalling act.

1111
01:19:48,200 --> 01:19:55,540
On February 26, 1993, a horrific act of international terrorism struck the United States.

1112
01:19:56,240 --> 01:19:58,580
In an attempt to bring down the Twin Towers,

1113
01:19:59,320 --> 01:20:02,140
Islamic terrorists called the Fifth Liberation Army

1114
01:20:02,141 --> 01:20:06,260
planted a bomb equivalent to 2,200 pounds of TNT

1115
01:20:06,261 --> 01:20:09,920
in the parking garage of the second floor basement of Tower 1

1116
01:20:09,921 --> 01:20:11,380
at the World Trade Center.

1117
01:20:12,020 --> 01:20:16,160
At 12.18 p.m., a huge explosion left six people dead

1118
01:20:16,161 --> 01:20:17,780
and more than 1,000 injured.

1119
01:20:19,520 --> 01:20:21,700
It was just a day that you don't forget.

1120
01:20:22,080 --> 01:20:23,520
I wasn't injured at all in the bombing.

1121
01:20:23,940 --> 01:20:26,060
I just was hit by a falling ceiling tile,

1122
01:20:26,220 --> 01:20:27,400
and I guess it wasn't my time.

1123
01:20:28,040 --> 01:20:30,840
I was about 150 feet from ground zero.

1124
01:20:30,841 --> 01:20:35,780
I took the elevators down to the B-2 level, was walking down the hallway that day, and

1125
01:20:35,781 --> 01:20:39,720
I just entered the door to my office, and the bomb went off.

1126
01:20:39,721 --> 01:20:46,000
All I remember is a tremendous loud sound and a blast of wind going behind me, and the

1127
01:20:46,001 --> 01:20:49,900
secretary that was walking with me that day, and she was heading down the hall to the carpenter's

1128
01:20:49,901 --> 01:20:50,900
office.

1129
01:20:50,901 --> 01:20:53,820
Both her legs were broken by this blast of wind.

1130
01:20:53,821 --> 01:20:55,880
She wasn't hit by anything other than the blast force.

1131
01:20:55,881 --> 01:21:00,500
And then within about a half a second, my mind had realized that was a bomb.

1132
01:21:00,560 --> 01:21:03,600
And that's what I yelled to everyone in the office, it's a bomb, get out.

1133
01:21:04,480 --> 01:21:07,380
The massive explosion ripped through the basement floor,

1134
01:21:07,940 --> 01:21:11,220
creating a hole 100 feet wide, four stories deep,

1135
01:21:11,640 --> 01:21:14,680
a tomb of crumpled cars, concrete, and twisted steel.

1136
01:21:15,780 --> 01:21:20,720
However, the apocalyptic devastation hoped for by the terrorists did not come to pass.

1137
01:21:21,280 --> 01:21:23,660
There was much damage caused by the explosion,

1138
01:21:23,661 --> 01:21:29,320
but it was all in the surrounding area and very little inside of the footprint of the building,

1139
01:21:29,600 --> 01:21:32,660
despite the fact that the bomb was placed immediately adjacent to it.

1140
01:21:33,740 --> 01:21:39,820
Fortunately, the World Trade Center had been constructed to withstand hurricane winds of up to 150 miles per hour.

1141
01:21:40,800 --> 01:21:44,600
The energy impact from those winds far exceeds the impact from the bombing.

1142
01:21:45,680 --> 01:21:50,360
While the bomb was similar to the one that completely destroyed the Murrah Building in Oklahoma City,

1143
01:21:50,361 --> 01:21:55,020
The steel vertical columns of the World Trade Center did not flinch.

1144
01:21:55,680 --> 01:22:05,760
An analogy that one might put to that is if you take a firecracker and you put the firecracker on the ground and you put a can over it.

1145
01:22:05,880 --> 01:22:10,400
Anyone who's done that realizes that the can goes shooting up in the air and the ground doesn't move.

1146
01:22:10,480 --> 01:22:13,120
Well, the building was analogous to the ground.

1147
01:22:13,280 --> 01:22:14,540
It didn't really move.

1148
01:22:14,620 --> 01:22:18,560
That wall was very strong relative to anything else around.

1149
01:22:18,561 --> 01:22:22,140
and so the force of the explosion, the shock wave,

1150
01:22:22,500 --> 01:22:24,380
basically hit that surface and reflected.

1151
01:22:25,700 --> 01:22:28,900
All that it caused was a crack in one of the support columns

1152
01:22:28,901 --> 01:22:31,420
that you could stick a dollar bill through on one side.

1153
01:22:31,760 --> 01:22:34,060
The other three sides of the column remained intact

1154
01:22:34,061 --> 01:22:38,060
and the loads just transferred to all the other structural steel in the complex.

1155
01:22:40,640 --> 01:22:42,640
What concerned engineers most

1156
01:22:42,641 --> 01:22:46,780
was the bathtub-like seven-story basement of the Trade Center complex

1157
01:22:46,781 --> 01:22:48,980
that keeps out the waters of New York Harbor.

1158
01:22:50,300 --> 01:22:53,220
The horizontal basement floors that formed the lateral support

1159
01:22:53,221 --> 01:22:55,020
for the bathtubs had been destroyed.

1160
01:22:56,600 --> 01:22:59,380
The loss of the basement floors meant that the support

1161
01:22:59,381 --> 01:23:01,100
of the bathtub walls was in question.

1162
01:23:01,880 --> 01:23:04,340
The entire wind load is carried to these columns.

1163
01:23:05,400 --> 01:23:08,760
New York's weather forecast presented an even greater sense of urgency.

1164
01:23:09,380 --> 01:23:12,200
There was concern, particularly since there was a windstorm predicted

1165
01:23:12,201 --> 01:23:14,280
for the following week, that some of these columns

1166
01:23:14,281 --> 01:23:18,220
might in fact get some additional load to the windstorm and would buckle.

1167
01:23:18,920 --> 01:23:20,300
Columns can be like rulers.

1168
01:23:20,480 --> 01:23:24,420
If you push on them enough, they'll just buckle out to the side at a given point.

1169
01:23:25,280 --> 01:23:29,760
Within 36 hours, the damaged structural column and remaining columns

1170
01:23:29,761 --> 01:23:31,600
were given solid additional bracing.

1171
01:23:32,520 --> 01:23:34,820
Cleaning the extensive debris was another priority.

1172
01:23:35,900 --> 01:23:40,840
It was a massive, almost like Normandy or D-Day.

1173
01:23:40,841 --> 01:23:43,820
The cleanup operation was on a heroic scale.

1174
01:23:43,821 --> 01:23:46,820
The engineering reconstruction was on a heroic scale.

1175
01:23:48,560 --> 01:23:53,740
Working round the clock, an army of engineers, maintenance people, and construction workers

1176
01:23:53,741 --> 01:23:56,500
removed over 6,000 tons of debris.

1177
01:23:57,480 --> 01:24:01,040
Final cleanup and repair costs reached more than $500 million.

1178
01:24:02,700 --> 01:24:06,940
Twenty days after the blast, the World Trade Center reopened its doors.

1179
01:24:07,560 --> 01:24:11,960
The first tenant to move back into his office was New York Governor Mario Cuomo.

1180
01:24:11,961 --> 01:24:16,960
It's clean, it's comfortable, don't worry about it.

1181
01:24:16,961 --> 01:24:20,960
New emergency communications were installed at key locations,

1182
01:24:20,961 --> 01:24:24,960
and increased security for truck deliveries and pickups were implemented.

1183
01:24:24,961 --> 01:24:27,960
Tenants and visitors conducting business at the World Trade Center

1184
01:24:27,961 --> 01:24:30,960
now wear electronic identification badges,

1185
01:24:30,961 --> 01:24:35,960
and access to the basement parking lots is restricted to tenants only.

1186
01:24:41,960 --> 01:24:45,120
There was a loss of life.

1187
01:24:45,121 --> 01:24:48,040
Some of my friends were killed in this incident.

1188
01:24:48,041 --> 01:24:52,260
But it shows the tremendous resiliency and strength of these two towers, that such a

1189
01:24:52,261 --> 01:25:01,360
significant event resulted in what was amazingly not that much property damage and what could

1190
01:25:01,361 --> 01:25:06,300
have been a real tragedy, only six people were killed here, rather than the tens of

1191
01:25:06,301 --> 01:25:08,680
thousands that the terrorists wanted to kill.

1192
01:25:08,681 --> 01:25:12,760
It was a symbolic act more than a murderous act.

1193
01:25:12,761 --> 01:25:19,780
The World Trade Center is a symbol, an icon, a representation, not just of the port of New

1194
01:25:19,781 --> 01:25:26,080
York and New Jersey or of Lower Manhattan or of Wall Street, but indeed of the capitalist

1195
01:25:26,081 --> 01:25:29,420
system and by extension, all of America.

1196
01:25:29,421 --> 01:25:34,120
Now more than ever before, the World Trade Center could be seen as a symbol of New York's

1197
01:25:34,121 --> 01:25:36,500
strength and resilience.

1198
01:25:36,501 --> 01:25:42,280
engineering projects like this become another way of our continuing to tell a story about

1199
01:25:42,281 --> 01:25:44,000
who and what we are.

1200
01:25:44,001 --> 01:25:47,740
You know, that's one of the ways that civilization reproduces itself.

1201
01:25:47,741 --> 01:25:52,940
And sometimes we have to write these things out real big and then look at all the small

1202
01:25:52,941 --> 01:25:56,160
stuff that goes into them, floor by floor by floor.

1203
01:26:06,500 --> 01:26:14,600
Since this show was produced, we had the opportunity to talk again with Professor Angus Gillespie

1204
01:26:14,601 --> 01:26:18,380
about his reaction to the terrible tragedy of September 11th.

1205
01:26:18,840 --> 01:26:21,000
That's coming up next, after the break.

